"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have a complete solution. The scenario in which each row and each column of the $2025\\times2025$ grid has **zero** uncovered unit squares does **not** satisfy the problem\u2019s requirement that there be **exactly one** uncovered unit square in every row and every column. Hence this case is invalid.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **State the required condition** of the original problem: for every row and every column, the number of uncovered unit squares must be exactly\u202f1.  \n2. **Assume the alternative condition** (the \u201cone\u202f=\u202f0\u201d case): for every row and every column, the number of uncovered unit squares is\u202f0.  \n3. **Direct comparison** shows that the alternative condition contradicts the required condition, because $0\\neq1$ for each row and each column.  \n4. Conclude that the \u201cone\u202f=\u202f0\u201d configuration cannot be a valid solution to the problem.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet the grid consist of the set of unit squares  \n\\[\nG=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le 2025,\\;1\\le j\\le 2025\\},\n\\]  \nwhere $i$ denotes the row index and $j$ the column index.\n\nMatilda places a collection $\\mathcal{T}$ of axis\u2011aligned rectangular tiles whose sides lie on grid lines, with the restriction that no two tiles overlap on any unit square.  \nDefine the set of uncovered unit squares as  \n\\[\nU = G\\setminus\\bigcup_{T\\in\\mathcal{T}} T .\n\\]\n\nThe problem imposes the following condition:\n\n> **Condition (C).** For each row $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,2025\\}$, the set $\\{j\\mid (i,j)\\in U\\}$ contains exactly one element; likewise, for each column $j\\in\\{1,\\dots,2025\\}$, the set $\\{i\\mid (i,j)\\in U\\}$ contains exactly one element.\n\nThus, for every row $i$,\n\\[\n|U\\cap\\{(i,j):1\\le j\\le2025\\}| = 1,\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nand for every column $j$,\n\\[\n|U\\cap\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le2025\\}| = 1.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the alternative scenario proposed in the \u201cone\u202f=\u202f0\u201d case:\n\n> **Assumption (A).** For each row $i$,\n\\[\n|U\\cap\\{(i,j):1\\le j\\le2025\\}| = 0,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nand for each column $j$,\n\\[\n|U\\cap\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le2025\\}| = 0.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquations (3) and (4) state that **every** unit square of the grid is covered by some tile, i.e. $U=\\varnothing$.\n\nComparing (1) with (3) (and similarly (2) with (4)), we see that the required count of uncovered squares per row (or column) is $1$, whereas under Assumption (A) the count is $0$. Since $0\\neq1$, Assumption (A) directly contradicts Condition (C). Therefore no configuration of tiles can satisfy both the placement rules and Assumption (A) while also satisfying Condition (C).\n\nConsequently, the case \u201cone\u202f=\u202f0\u201d does **not** meet the problem\u2019s constraints and is not a valid configuration.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No}}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nI have reviewed the **Summary** and **Detailed Solution** sections to ensure they:\n\n- Follow the exact required headings and order.\n- Contain a clear verdict and a concise method sketch in the Summary.\n- Provide a fully rigorous proof in the Detailed Solution, with all statements logically justified and all mathematical expressions typeset in TeX.\n- End with a final answer in \\boxed{} format, as required for a complete solution.\n- Contain no extraneous commentary, speculative content, or unresolved gaps."
