\\(L_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) and \\(R_j=(j,\\sigma(j)+1)\\).  \nLet the column interval of \\(T\\) be \\([c,d]\\).  \nBecause \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\in[c,d]\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)+1\\in[c,d]\\), the interval \\([c,d]\\) contains the whole integer segment between them, in particular the column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) (if \\(\\sigma(i)\\le\\sigma(j)\\)) or \\(\\sigma(j)\\) (if \\(\\sigma(j)\\le\\sigma(i)\\)).  \nSince the corresponding row is also in the row interval of \\(T\\), the uncovered cell \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) or \\((j,\\sigma(j))\\) lies inside \\(T\\), a contradiction. \u220e\n\n---\n\nFrom Lemmas\u202f1\u20133 we deduce:\n\n* The \\(n-1\\) left\u2011adjacent cells must lie in pairwise distinct tiles.\n* The \\(n-1\\) right\u2011adjacent cells must also lie in pairwise distinct tiles.\n* No tile can serve simultaneously for a left\u2011adjacent cell and a right\u2011adjacent cell.\n\nHence any tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles. For \\(n=2025\\) this lower bound equals \\(4048\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n*Upper triangular region.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place a tile  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{up}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the rectangle with top\u2011left corner \\((i,i+1)\\) and bottom\u2011right corner \\((i,n)\\).  \n\\(T_i^{\\text{up}}\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle and contains no diagonal cell.\n\n*Lower triangular region.*  \nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place a tile  \n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{down}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the rectangle with top\u2011left corner \\((j+1,j)\\) and bottom\u2011right corner \\((n,j)\\).  \n\\(T_j^{\\text{down}}\\) is a \\((n-j)\\times 1\\) rectangle and also avoids all diagonal cells.\n\nThe families \\(\\{T_i^{\\text{up}}\\}_