olumns. So the induction works. The base case n=2: they argue that the two uncovered squares are at (1,2) and (2,1) (i.e., sigma(1)=2, sigma(2)=1). Then each of the remaining squares must be covered individually by a tile (size 1x1). So at least 2 tiles. Indeed 2*2-2=2. So lower bound holds.\n\nNow the construction: they choose sigma(i)=i (main diagonal). Then they define horizontal rectangles H_i covering row i, columns i+1..n (i.e., the cells to the right of the diagonal). These are 1 x (n-i) rectangles. And vertical rectangles V_j covering column j, rows j+1..n (i.e., cells below the diagonal). These are (n-j) x 1 rectangles. They cover all off-diagonal cells exactly once, leaving diagonal cells uncovered. So total number of rectangles = (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. So the construction works. So the answer 2n-2 is correct.\n\nThus answer 4048 is correct.\n\nNow we need to check reasoning validity. The solution uses induction (valid method), case analysis (selecting column 1), explicit construction (explicit rectangles). All are valid methods. So reasoning is valid.\n\nNow we need to check presentation quality. Are there any justification gaps or critical errors? Let's examine the solution.\n\nThe solution is fairly detailed. However, there might be some minor gaps: For base case n=2, they claim \"the uncovered squares are (1,2) and (2,1) (up to relabelling). These two squares are not adjacent, so a rectangle containing both would be the whole 2x2 board and would contain uncovered squares. Hence each of the two remaining squares must be covered by its own tile.\" This is correct. Might be a small gap: they assume sigma(1)=2, sigma(2)=1 after relabeling; but any permutation of size 2 has that property (the only possibilities are identity (1,1) and (2,2) or swapped (1,2) and (2,1). Actually for n=2, the permutation could be identity: uncovered squares (1,1) and (2,2). In that case, the two remaining squares are (1,2) and (2,1), which are not adjacent either? Actually they are 