 i\\le n-1)\\) set  \n\n     \\[\n     H_i=\\{(i,j):j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     a horizontal strip of height\u202f1.\n\n   The families \\(\\{V_j\\}\\) and \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, contain no hole, and together cover every cell of \\(L\\cup R\\).  Hence they form a tiling with exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   tiles.  For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(4048\\).\n\n5. **Maximum.**  \n   Using a unit\u2011square tile for each covered cell yields \\(n^2-n=n(n-1)=4\\,098\\,600\\) tiles.\n\n6. **All intermediate values are achievable.**  \n   Starting from the minimal tiling above, pick any rectangle of size at least\u202f2 and split it along a grid line into two smaller rectangles.  The split increases the tile count by exactly\u202f1 while preserving a valid tiling (the new rectangles still lie wholly in \\(L\\) or \\(R\\) and contain no hole).  \n   The minimal tiling contains many rectangles of size\u202f>\u202f1 (all \\(V_j\\) with \\(j\\le n-2\\) and all \\(H_i\\) with \\(i\\le n-2\\)), so we can perform a split as long as the current tiling is not already the all\u2011unit\u2011square tiling.  Repeating this operation we can increase the number of tiles from the minimum \\(2n-2\\) up to the maximum \\(n(n-1)\\) one by one.  Therefore every integer  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\quad\\text{with}\\quad 2n-2\\le k\\le n(n-1)\n   \\]\n\n   occurs as the number of tiles of some admissible tiling.\n\n   Substituting \\(n=2025\\) gives the full attainable set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z\\mid 4048\\le k\\le 4\\,098\\,600\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\nThus the minimum is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\) and all intermediate values up to the obvious maximum are realizable.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  The board consists of the unit squares  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(i,j):1\\le i,j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nMatilda places axis\u2011parallel rectangular tiles whose sides lie on grid lines; a tile is a set  \n\n\\[\nT=I\\times J,\\qquad I,J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are intervals of integers.  Tiles are required to be pairwise disjoint and to cover all squares of \\(B\\) except exactly one square in each row and each column.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Permutation description of the uncovered squares\n\nSince each row contains exactly one uncovered square, there is a function  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  Because each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\sigma\\) must be a bijection; i.e. a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nFor a given permutation \\(\\sigma\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\in B:j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\in B:j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L\\) consists of the squares lying strictly to the left of the hole in their row, and \\(R\\) consists of the squares lying strictly to the right.  The set of covered squares is exactly \\(L\\cup R\\).\n\nA tile may not contain a hole, therefore every tile is wholly contained either in \\(L\\) or in \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the number of tiles\n\nWe prove that each of the regions \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles in any partition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma\u202f1.**  The region \\(L\\) cannot be partitioned into fewer than \\(n-1\\) axis\u2011parallel rectangles.\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*  \n\n*Base case \\(n=1\\).*  The board consists of a single square, which is uncovered; thus \\(L=\\varnothing\\) and the statement is vacuous.\n\n*Inductive step.*  Assume the lemma holds for all sizes \\(<n\\) and consider a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nLet \\(i^{*}\\) be the unique index with \\(\\sigma(i^{*})=n\\) (the hole in the last column).  Then  \n\n\\[\nL_{i^{*}}=\\{(i^{*},j):1\\le j\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor any other row \\(i\\neq i^{*}\\) we have \\(\\sigma(i)\\le n-1\\), whence  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j\\le\\sigma(i)-1\\}\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the cell \\((i^{*},n-1)\\) belongs to \\(L\\) but to no other \\(L_i\\).  Any rectangle of a partition of \\(L\\) that contains \\((i^{*},n-1)\\) cannot contain any row different from \\(i^{*}\\); otherwise it would also contain a cell \\((i,n-1)\\) with \\(i\\neq i^{*}\\), which is not in \\(L\\).  Hence at least one rectangle of the partition is confined to row \\(i^{*}\\).\n\nDelete row \\(i^{*}\\) and column \\(n\\) from the board.  The remaining left\u2011side region is  \n\n\\[\nL'=\\{(i,j):i\\neq i^{*},\\;j<\\sigma(i)\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich corresponds to the permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) obtained from \\(\\sigma\\) by deleting the entry \\(n\\).  By the induction hypothesis, \\(L'\\) requires at least \\((n-1)-1=n-2\\) rectangles.  Adding the rectangle forced for row \\(i^{*}\\) yields a total of at least \\((n-2)+1=n-1\\) rectangles for \\(L\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma\u202f2.**  The region \\(R\\) cannot be partitioned into fewer than \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  Apply Lemma\u202f1 to the permutation \\(\\sigma^{-1}\\) (which interchanges the roles of rows and columns).  Equivalently, let \\(i^{\\dagger}\\) be the row with \\(\\sigma(i^{\\dagger})=1\\); the cell \\((i^{\\dagger},2)\\) belongs only to the right\u2011side part of row \\(i^{\\dagger}\\), forcing a rectangle confined to that row.  Removing row \\(i^{\\dagger}\\) and column\u202f1 reduces the problem to size \\(n-1\\).  The same induction as in Lemma\u202f1 gives the bound \\(n-1\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 we obtain the universal lower bound\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any admissible tiling uses at least }2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A tiling attaining the lower bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  Then the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define the vertical strip  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j):i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_j\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\); it lies entirely in the left side because every cell of \\(V_j\\) satisfies \\(j< i\\).\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define the horizontal strip  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j):j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_i\\) is a rectangle of size \\(1\\times(n-i)\\); it lies entirely in the right side because every cell of \\(H_i\\) satisfies \\(j> i\\).\n\n*Disjointness.*  \nTwo distinct vertical strips \\(V_j,V_{j'}\\) have different column indices, hence are disjoint.  Two distinct horizontal strips \\(H_i,H_{i'}\\) have different row indices, hence are disjoint.  Moreover \\(V_j\\cap H_i=\\varnothing\\) because a point of \\(V_j\\) has column \\(j\\) while a point of \\(H_i\\) has column \\(>i\\); the inequalities \\(j<i\\) (since \\(i\\ge j+1\\) for any point of \\(V_j\\)) and \\(j>i\\) (required for a point of \\(H_i\\)) cannot hold simultaneously.\n\n*Covering.*  \nLet \\((i,j)\\) be a covered square, i.e. \\(j\\neq i\\).\n\n- If \\(j<i\\) then \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,i-1\\}\\) and \\((i,j)\\in V_j\\) because \\(i\\ge j+1\\).\n- If \\(j>i\\) then \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,j-1\\}\\) and \\((i,j)\\in H_i\\) because \\(j\\ge i+1\\).\n\nThus every covered square belongs to exactly one of the rectangles \\(\\{V_j\\}\\cup\\{H_i\\}\\).\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T=\\{V_j:1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{H_i:1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nis a tiling of the board satisfying the required condition, and  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) this yields \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Maximum possible number of tiles\n\nIf every covered unit square is taken as a separate tile, we obtain a tiling with  \n\n\\[\nn^2-n=n(n-1)\n\\]\n\ntiles.  For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2025\\cdot2024=4\\,098\\,600\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  All intermediate numbers are attainable\n\nStart from the tiling \\(\\mathcal T\\) of (3).  Any rectangle in \\(\\mathcal T\\) that contains at least two unit squares can be split along a grid line into two smaller rectangles, each still lying wholly in \\(L\\) or in \\(R\\).  Such a split increases the total number of tiles by exactly one and preserves all required properties (disjointness, no holes).\n\nIn \\(\\mathcal T\\) the rectangles \\(V_j\\) with \\(j\\le n-2\\) have height \\(n-j\\ge2\\), and the rectangles \\(H_i\\) with \\(i\\le n-2\\) have width \\(n-i\\ge2\\).  Hence, as long as the current tiling is not the all\u2011unit\u2011square tiling, there exists at least one rectangle of size \\(\\ge2\\) that can be split.\n\nPerforming a split repeatedly we can increase the number of tiles from the minimum \\(2n-2\\) up to the maximum \\(n(n-1)\\) one by one.  Therefore for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\quad\\text{with}\\quad 2n-2\\le k\\le n(n-1)\n\\]\n\nthere exists an admissible tiling using exactly \\(k\\) tiles.\n\nSubstituting \\(n=2025\\) gives the complete set of attainable numbers:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z\\mid 4048\\le k\\le 4\\,098\\,600\\,\\}}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe minimum number of tiles required is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).  \nMoreover, every integer between \\(4048\\) and \\(4\\,098\\,600\\) inclusive can be realized as the number of tiles in a valid placement.\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing, verify that:\n\n- The permutation model correctly captures the \u201cexactly one uncovered square per row and column\u201d.\n- Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 are proved by valid induction and correctly handle the base case.\n- The construction using the vertical strips \\(V_j\\) and horizontal strips \\(H_i\\) indeed consists of disjoint rectangles, covers all non\u2011hole squares, and contains no hole.\n- The counting of tiles in the construction yields \\(2n-2\\).\n- The splitting argument truly allows an increase of the tile count by exactly one at each step and can be performed until all tiles are unit squares, thereby covering every integer between the bounds.\n- Numerical values for \\(n=2025\\) (minimum \\(4048\\), maximum \\(4\\,098\\,600\\)) are computed correctly.\n\nIf any of these points are inaccurate, revise the corresponding part of the solution. Ensure all mathematics is enclosed in TeX delimiters and that the output follows the required section order."
