region is tiled by rectangles whose column interval is a suffix \\(\\{c,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the left region  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\sigma(i)-1\\qquad (0\\le L_i\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); in particular the **positive** values \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) each occur exactly once.\n\nFor a column index \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_c=\\{\\,i\\mid L_i\\ge c\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe left region consists precisely of the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j\\le L_i\\); consequently column \\(c\\) of the left region is covered exactly by the rows in \\(S_c\\).\n\nObserve that the family \\(\\{S_c\\}_{c=1}^{n-1}\\) is strictly decreasing:\n\\[\nS_1\\supset S_2\\supset\\cdots\\supset S_{n-1},\n\\]\nand the inclusions are proper because each positive integer \\(c\\) occurs as some \\(L_i\\). Hence there are **\\(n-1\\) distinct** sets among the \\(S_c\\).\n\nNow consider any partition of \\(L\\) into rectangles of the form (3).  \nEach such rectangle has a column interval \\(\\{1,\\dots ,c\\}\\) for some \\(c\\). All columns belonging to the same rectangle are covered by the same set of rows (the rectangle’s row set). Consequently a rectangle can realize at most one transition from one set \\(S_c\\) to the next smaller set \\(S_{c+1}\\). Since there are \\(n-1\\) distinct sets, at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover the left region.\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any tiling of }L\\text{ uses at least }n-1\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the right region  \n\nDefine the *right length* of row \\(i\\) by  \n\n\\[\nR_i=n-\\sigma(i)\\qquad (0\\le R_i\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nExactly as in the left case, the multiset \\(\\{R_i\\}\\) is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\). Repeating the argument of Section 3 with the sets  \n\n\\[\nT_c=\\{\\,i\\mid R_i\\ge c\\,\\},\\qquad c=1,\\dots ,n-1,\n\\]\