ss the uncovered squares? Let's examine: Suppose tile covers rows i and i' and columns j and j' (j <= j'). If sigma(i) > j (so J(T) subset of columns less than sigma(i)?) Actually, the condition is that sigma(i) not in J(T). So sigma(i) cannot be equal to any column in J(T). But sigma(i) could be less than some columns in J(T) and greater than others. For a given tile covering multiple rows, it's possible that for some row i, sigma(i) is less than some columns in J(T) (i.e., tile extends to the right of sigma(i)), while for another row i', sigma(i') is greater than some columns in J(T) (i.e., tile extends to the left of sigma(i')). That would mean tile straddles the uncovered squares in different rows. But is that allowed? The tile is a rectangle, so its column range is contiguous. If for some row i, sigma(i) is less than the leftmost column of the tile, then tile is entirely to the right of sigma(i). If for some row i', sigma(i') is greater than the rightmost column of the tile, then tile is entirely to the left of sigma(i'). But if both happen, then the tile's column range must be both to the right of sigma(i) and to the left of sigma(i'), which is possible only if sigma(i) < leftmost column <= rightmost column < sigma(i'). That would mean sigma(i) < sigma(i'), and tile's columns are between them. But then the tile would not contain any uncovered squares because uncovered squares are at (i, sigma(i)) and (i', sigma(i')). The tile lies in columns strictly between sigma(i) and sigma(i'), but for row i, those columns are to the right of sigma(i); for row i', those columns are to the left of sigma(i'). So tile does not contain any uncovered squares. So the tile could be placed in the \"middle region\" between sigma(i) and sigma(i') for rows i and i'. However, the solution's claim is that any tile must lie entirely in L or R, i.e., either all columns less than sigma(i) for all rows i in I(T) or all columns greater than sigma(i) for all rows i in I(T). But the above scenario shows a tile could be in the \"middle region\" between sigma(i) and sigma(i') for rows i and i' where sigma(i) < sigma(i'), and tile's columns are between them. But does that contradict the condition that sigma(i) not in J(T) for each i? No, because sigma(i) is not in J(T) (the tile's column range does not include sigma(i)), and sigma(i') not in J(T). So the tile's columns are a set J(T) that is a subset of columns that are all greater than sigma(i) (for row i) and all less than sigma(i') (for row i'). So J(T) is not necessarily a subset of columns less than min sigma(i) (which would be sigma(i) if sigma(i) is the minimum among sigma(i) for i in I(T)). Actually, the solution's argument says: \"Because a tile may not contain an uncovered square, for every i in I(T) we have sigma(i) not in J(T). Consequently either J(T) subset of {1,..., min_i sigma(i)-1} or J(T) subset of {max_i sigma(i)+1,..., n}.\" This is not necessarily true. Let's examine: Suppose I(T) = {i1,i2} with sigma(i1) = 3, sigma(i2) = 7. Then min sigma = 3, max sigma = 7. The tile's columns could be {4,5,6}, which is not a subset of {1,...,2} (i.e., less than min sigma) nor a subset of {8,...,n} (greater than max sigma). So the tile's columns are between min sigma and max sigma. This would violate the claim that J(T) must be either entirely left of all sigma(i) or entirely right of all sigma(i). But is such a tile allowed? Let's check the geometry: The tile covers rows i1 and i2, and columns 4-6. For row i1 (sigma=3), the tile's columns are to the right of sigma(i1). For row i2 (sigma=7), the tile's columns are to the left of sigma(i2). The tile is a rectangle covering rows i1,i2 and columns 4-6. Does it intersect any uncovered squares? The uncovered squares are at (i1,3) and (i2,7). The tile does not cover those squares. So it seems allowed. So the solution's claim that each tile must be entirely left or right of all uncovered squares is false. However, we need to check if the tile can have rows 