nbe the number of tiles that contain this row; define similarly  \n\n\\[\nb_j=\\#\\{k\\mid j\\in C_k\\}\n\\]\n\nfor columns.\nSumming (2) over all tiles gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{m}\\bigl(|R_k|+|C_k|\\bigr)\\le mn . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBut the left\u2011hand side of (3) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i+\\sum_{j=1}^{n}b_j . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nWe now obtain a **lower** bound for the sum (4).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \n\nFor every row \\(i\\) we have \\(a_i\\ge 1\\) and, moreover,  \n\n\\[\na_i+b_{\\pi(i)}\\ge 2 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nRow \\(i\\) contains the \\(n-1\\) squares that are not the hole \\((i,\\pi(i))\\);\nthey must be covered, therefore at least one tile contains the row,\nso \\(a_i\\ge1\\).  \nThe column \\(\\pi(i)\\) contains the hole \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) and therefore cannot\nappear together with row \\(i\\) in any tile. Consequently the column\n\\(\\pi(i)\\) must be covered by tiles **different** from those covering row\n\\(i\\); hence at least one tile contains the column \\(\\pi(i)\\), i.e.\n\\(b_{\\pi(i)}\\ge1\\). Adding the two inequalities yields (5). \u220e\n\n\n\nSummation of (5) over all \\(i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i+\\sum_{i=1}^{n}b_{\\pi(i)}\\ge 2n .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, \\(\\{b_{\\pi(i)}\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}=\n\\{b_j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i+\\sum_{j=1}^{n}b_j\\ge 2n . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3), (4) and (6) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nmn\\;\\ge\\; \\sum_{k=1}^{m}\\bigl(|R_k|+|C_k|\\bigr)\n          \\;=\\; \\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i+\\sum_{j=1}^{n}b_j\n          \\;\\ge\\; 2n .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge \\frac{2n}{n}=2 .\n\\]\n\nThis inequality is far from what we need; we must sharpen it.\nThe improvement comes from observing that (5) is actually strict for\n**all but two** rows (and columns).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \n\nExactly two indices \\(i\\) satisfy \\(a_i=1\\) and \\(b_{\\pi(i)}=1\\); for every\nother index \\(i\\) we have  \n\n\\[\na_i+b_{\\pi(i)}\\ge 3 . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that for some row \\(i\\) we have \\(a_i=1\\) and \\(b_{\\pi(i)}=1\\).\nLet the unique tile containing row \\(i\\) be \\(T(R,C)\\).  \nBecause \\(i\\in R\\) we have \\(\\pi(i)\\notin C\\) (by (1)), therefore the\nunique tile containing column \\(\\pi(i)\\) cannot be the same tile; it must be a\ndifferent tile \\(T(R',C')\\) with \\(\\pi(i)\\in C'\\) and \\(i\\notin R'\\).\n\nNow look at any other row \\(k\\neq i\\).  \nIf the tile that contains row \\(k\\) also contained column \\(\\pi(i)\\) then,\ntogether with the tile that contains column \\(\\pi(i)\\), the two tiles would\nshare the square \\((k,\\pi(i))\\), contradicting the disjointness of the\ntiling. Consequently **every** tile that contains a row different from \\(i\\)\nmust avoid the column \\(\\pi(i)\\). Hence for each such row \\(k\\) the column set\nof its tile is a subset of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\pi(i)\\}\\), whose size is\n\\(n-1\\). By (1) this forces the tile containing row \\(k\\) to have at most\n\\(n-1\\) columns, i.e. it cannot be the \u201cfull\u2011row\u2019\u2019 tile that would give\n\\(a_k=1\\) and \\(b_{\\pi(k)}=1\\). Consequently for every \\(k\\neq i\\) we must\nhave either \\(a_k\\ge2\\) or \\(b_{\\pi(k)}\\ge2\\), which yields (7). \u220e\n\n\n\nSumming (7) over all \\(i\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i+\\sum_{j=1}^{n}b_j\n   \\;=\\; \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl(a_i+b_{\\pi(i)}\\bigr)\n   \\;\\ge\\; 2\\cdot 2 + (n-2)\\cdot 3\n   \\;=\\; 2n+ (n-2) .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{m}\\bigl(|R_k|+|C_k|\\bigr)\\ge 2n+(n-2). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2) we have \\(|R_k|+|C_k|\\le n\\) for each tile, so from (8)\n\n\\[\nmn \\;\\ge\\; 2n+(n-2)\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nm\\;\\ge\\; 2+\\frac{n-2}{n}=2+\\!1-\\frac{2}{n}=2n-2\\;\\frac{2}{n}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\) is an integer, the inequality forces  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge 2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  A construction attaining \\(2n-2\\)\n\nTake the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place two tiles:\n\n* a **horizontal** tile covering row \\(i\\) and the columns \\(i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\);\n  i.e. \\(T(\\{i\\},\\{i+1,\\dots ,n\\})\\);\n* a **vertical** tile covering column \\(i\\) and the rows \\(i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\);\n  i.e. \\(T(\\{i+1,\\dots ,n\\},\\{i\\})\\).\n\nThese \\(2(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint, they cover every\nsquare except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), and each row and each column\ncontains exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal one).  \nHence a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown that any admissible tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n(Lemmas\u202f1\u20132) and that a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists (the\nconstruction above). Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2n-2}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\nThus Matilda can achieve the required condition with 4048 tiles, and it is\nimpossible to do it with fewer. \u220e"
