"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Structure of the uncovered squares.**  \n   The condition \u201cexactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column\u2019\u2019 forces the uncovered squares to form a permutation matrix: there is a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (\\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\n2. **Decomposition into left and right parts.**  \n   For each row \\(i\\) let  \n   \\[\n   L_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad \n   R_i=\\{(i,j):\\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n   \\]  \n   The set \\(L=\\bigcup_i L_i\\) consists of all squares left of the holes, and \\(R=\\bigcup_i R_i\\) consists of all squares right of the holes.  \n   A rectangle cannot contain a hole, hence every tile lies entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).\n\n3. **Lower bound for the left part.**  \n   For each column \\(k\\;(1\\le k\\le n-1)\\) there is a unique row \\(i_k\\) with \\(\\sigma(i_k)=k+1\\).  \n   The cell \\((i_k,k)\\) belongs to \\(L\\). Any rectangle covering this cell cannot contain column \\(k+1\\) (it is a hole in that row), so its **rightmost column** is exactly \\(k\\).  \n   Since a rectangle has a single rightmost column, distinct values of \\(k\\) require distinct rectangles.  \n   Hence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(L\\).\n\n4. **Lower bound for the right part.**  \n   By symmetry, for each column \\(k\\;(2\\le k\\le n)\\) there is a unique row \\(j_k\\) with \\(\\sigma(j_k)=k-1\\).  \n   The cell \\((j_k,k)\\) belongs to \\(R\\). Any rectangle covering it cannot contain column \\(k-1\\), so its **leftmost column** is exactly \\(k\\).  \n   Distinct values of \\(k\\) again force distinct rectangles, giving at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles for \\(R\\).\n\n5. **Total lower bound.**  \n   Any admissible tiling uses at least  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n   tiles.\n\n6. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).  \n   For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define two rectangles  \n   \\[\n   L_k=\\{(i,j):k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\},\\qquad \n   R_k=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n-k,\\;n-k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n   \\]  \n   \u2013 \\(L_k\\) is a \\((n-k)\\times k\\) rectangle lying entirely left of the diagonal.  \n   \u2013 \\(R_k\\) is a \\((n-k)\\times k\\) rectangle lying entirely right of the diagonal.  \n\n   The families \\(\\{L_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover every square of the board except the diagonal.  \n   Thus the board is tiled with exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles, meeting the lower bound.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n---  \n\n**### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of rectangular tiles is required to satisfy:\n\n* each tile is an axis\u2011parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines,\n* no unit square is covered by more than one tile,\n* in every row and in every column exactly one unit square is left uncovered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) let \\(\\sigma(i)\\) denote the column of the unique uncovered square in that row.  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the mapping \\(i\\mapsto\\sigma(i)\\) is a bijection of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); i.e. \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Left and right parts of the board  \n\nDefine, for each row \\(i\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad \nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\\qquad R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]\n\nEvery unit square that must be covered belongs either to \\(L\\) (to the left of its row\u2019s hole) or to \\(R\\) (to the right of its row\u2019s hole).  \n\nA rectangle cannot contain a hole; consequently a tile is contained entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).  \nThus the total number of tiles equals the number of rectangles needed to tile \\(L\\) plus the number needed to tile \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the left part  \n\nFor each column \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) there is a unique row  \n\n\\[\ni_k\\quad\\text{such that}\\quad\\sigma(i_k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe cell \\((i_k,k)\\) lies in \\(L\\) and is the rightmost cell of its row before the hole.\n\nLet \\(B\\) be any rectangle that covers \\((i_k,k)\\).  \nSince column \\(k+1\\) is a hole in row \\(i_k\\), the rectangle cannot contain column \\(k+1\\); therefore its rightmost column is at most \\(k\\).  \nBecause the rectangle does contain column \\(k\\), its rightmost column is **exactly** \\(k\\).\n\nHence each column \\(k\\) forces the existence of a rectangle whose rightmost column equals \\(k\\).  \nA rectangle possesses a single rightmost column, so two different values of \\(k\\) cannot be realised by the same rectangle.  \nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) distinct rectangles are required to cover all cells of \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the right part  \n\nThe argument for \\(R\\) is symmetric.  \nFor each column \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) there is a unique row  \n\n\\[\nj_k\\quad\\text{such that}\\quad\\sigma(j_k)=k-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe cell \\((j_k,k)\\) belongs to \\(R\\) and is the leftmost cell of its row after the hole.\n\nIf a rectangle \\(C\\) covers \\((j_k,k)\\), it cannot contain column \\(k-1\\) (a hole), so its leftmost column is at least \\(k\\).  \nSince the rectangle does contain column \\(k\\), its leftmost column is **exactly** \\(k\\).\n\nThus each column \\(k\\) (\\(2\\le k\\le n\\)) forces a rectangle whose leftmost column equals \\(k\\).  \nDistinct columns require distinct rectangles, giving at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles for \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Total lower bound  \n\nAny admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), one per row and column.\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) define two rectangles:\n\n\\[\nL_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\},\n\\qquad\nR_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-k,\\;n-k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n* \\(L_k\\) is a \\((n-k)\\times k\\) rectangle lying completely in the left part \\(L\\).\n* \\(R_k\\) is a \\((n-k)\\times k\\) rectangle lying completely in the right part \\(R\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{L_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint.  \nEvery cell of \\(L\\) belongs to exactly one \\(L_k\\): a cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\) lies in \\(L_j\\) because \\(j\\le i-1\\) implies \\(i\\ge j+1\\).  \nSimilarly, every cell of \\(R\\) belongs to exactly one \\(R_k\\): a cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) lies in \\(R_{\\,n-j}\\).  \n\nHence the board is partitioned into precisely \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles, and each row and column contains exactly one uncovered diagonal cell.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Minimality  \n\nThe construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, matching the lower bound proved in Sections\u202f3\u20135.  \nTherefore no placement can use fewer tiles, and the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
