n \\(R\\subseteq A\\) or \\(R\\subseteq B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the row interval of \\(R\\) as \\([c,d]\\) and its column interval as \\([a,b]\\) (both inclusive).  \nFor each column \\(j\\in[a,b]\\) the hole lies at row \\(r_j\\).  \nSince \\(R\\) does not contain \\((r_j,j)\\), either \\(d\\le r_j-1\\) (the rectangle stays above the hole) or \\(c\\ge r_j+1\\) (the rectangle stays below the hole).  \nIf there existed columns \\(j_1,j_2\\in[a,b]\\) with \\(d\\le r_{j_1}-1\\) and \\(c\\ge r_{j_2}+1\\), then the interval \\([c,d]\\) would be non‑empty and would contain some integer \\(t\\) with \\(r_{j_2}<t<r_{j_1}\\).  \nBecause the rows \\(\\{r_j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\}\\) form a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), there is a column \\(j\\) for which \\(r_j=t\\); then \\((t,j)\\in R\\), contradicting the hypothesis.  \nHence the same inequality holds for all columns in \\([a,b]\\), and \\(R\\) is wholly above (in \\(A\\)) or wholly below (in \\(B\\)). ∎\n\nConsequently the whole tiling is the disjoint union of a tiling of \\(A\\) and a tiling of \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimal number of rectangles for a histogram\n\n> **Lemma 2.**  \n> Let a histogram have column heights \\(h_1,\\dots ,h_n\\) with \\(h_0:=0\\).  \n> Any partition of this histogram into axis‑aligned rectangles uses at least  \n> \\[\n> \\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,\\,h_j-h_{j-1}\\}\n> \\tag{1}\n> \\]\n> rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the leftmost column where the height increases, i.e. a column \\(j\\) with\n\\(h_j>h_{j-1}\\).  \nThe \\(h_j-h_{j-1}\\) newly appearing unit squares in column \\(j\\) have no\nsquare to their left, so no rectangle that already started in a previous column can cover any of them.  \nHence each of those new squares must be covered by a rectangle whose leftmost column is exactly \\(j\\).  \nA single rectangle can contain at most one of the new rows (otherwise it would also contain squares in column \\(j-1\\) that are absent there).  \nTherefore at least \\(h_j-h_{j-1}\\) distinct rectangles must start at column \\(j\\).  \nSumming over all columns yields the bound (1). ∎\n\nApplying Lemma 2 to the upper region \\(A\\) (with heights \\(h_j=r_j-1\\)) and to the lower region \\(B\\) (with heights \\(\\ell_j=n-r_j\\)) gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_A &\\ge \\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,\\,r_j-r_{j-1}\\},\\\\[2mm]\nN_B &\\ge \\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,\\,(n-r_j)-(n-r_{j-1})\\}\n      =\\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,\\,r_{j-1}-r_j\\},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwhere we set \\(r_0:=1\\) for the upper sum and \\(r_0:=n\\) for the lower sum.\nAdding the two inequalities and observing that\n\\[\n\\max\\{0,\\,r_j-r_{j-1}\\}+\\max\\{0,\\,r_{j-1}-r_j\\}=|r_j-r_{j-1}|\n\\]\nyields\n\\[\nN\\;=\\;N_A+N_B\\;\\ge\\;(r_1-1)+(n-r_1)+\\sum_{j=2}^{n}|r_j-r_{j-1}|\n      \\;=\\;(n-1)+\\sum_{j=2}^{n}|r_j-r_{j-1}|.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the permutation variation\n\n> **Lemma 3.**  \n> For any permutation \\((r_1,\\dots ,r_n)\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\),\n> \\[\n> \\sum_{j=2}^{n}|r_j-r_{j-1}|\\;\\ge\\;n-1,\n> \\]\n> and equality holds precisely when the permutation is monotone increasing\n> \\((1,2,\\dots ,n)\\) or monotone decreasing \\((n,n-1,\\dots ,1)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the permutation is not monotone, there exists an index \\(k\\) with either\n\\(r_{k-1}<r_k>r_{k+1}\\) (a peak) or \\(r_{k-1}>r_k<r_{k+1}\\) (a valley).  \nSwapping the entries \\(r_k\\) and \\(r_{k+1}\\) reduces the sum\n\\(\\sum|r_{j+1}-r_j|\\) by at least \\(2\\).  \nRepeating such swaps eventually yields a monotone permutation, and each swap never increases the sum.  \nHence the minimum of the sum is attained by a monotone permutation.  \nFor the monotone increasing permutation we have\n\\(\\sum_{j=2}^{n}|r_j-r_{j-1}|=\\sum_{j=2}^{n}(j-(j-1))=n-1\\); the same holds for the decreasing one. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The lower bound for the number of tiles\n\nInsert Lemma 3 into inequality (2):\n\\[\nN\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus **any** tiling satisfying the problem’s condition uses at least\n\\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  A tiling with exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles\n\nTake the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main diagonal\n\\((i,i)\\), \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).\n\n*Horizontal tiles.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place the rectangle\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile.\n\n*Vertical tiles.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the rectangle\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich is an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) tile.\n\nThe sets \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint,\nthey cover every square except the diagonal, and each row and each column\ncontains exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal one).  \nHence this construction uses exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Conclusion\n\nFrom (3) we have the universal lower bound \\(N\\ge 2(n-1)\\); the construction above shows that this bound is attainable.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\\[\nN_{\\min}=2(n-1).\n\\]\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\\[\nN_{\\min}=2\\cdot(2025-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
