rete specification after the claim?\"\n  - If Category A or B (zero details/type only) → INVALID METHOD → FAIL Level 2\n  - If Category C (one or more concrete details) → VALID METHOD → PASS Level 2, proceed to Level 3\n\n- **Level 3 (Presentation Quality) asks:** \"Are the provided details COMPLETE?\"\n  - Justification gaps (incomplete details, missing steps) are Level 3 issues\n  - Do NOT reject at Level 2 if ANY concrete specification was provided\n\n**Examples Showing the Boundary:**\n\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 works\" (nothing follows - Category A)\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 using vertical lines\" (type named, but NO concrete details - Category B)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 works: L₁: y=x, L₂: y=2x\" (has equations - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 via lines at points (1,1), (2,2), (3,3)\" (has points - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Sunny line through (n,1)\" (one point provided - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Three sunny lines cover 6 rightmost points\" (count+target - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n\n**WARNING - Common Misclassification:**\n- If solution says \"k=X works\" AND THEN provides equations/points/count → This is Category C (PASS Level 2)\n- Only reject at Level 2 if \"k=X works\" appears WITH NO concrete details following it\n- ONE point is better than ZERO points → proceed to Level 3 for completeness check\n\n**STEP 4: Handle Context-Dependent Claims (CRITICAL CALIBRATION)**\n\nSome mathematical claims are true in one context but false in another. Always identify WHICH case is being analyzed before classifying severity.\n\n**Example Pattern:**\n```\nClaim: \"The three rightmost columns force the use of a vertical line for column n-2\"\nContext A (k≤3): This claim is TRUE\nContext B (k≥4): This claim is FALSE\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≤3 case:\n  → Claim is TRUE in this context\n  → If scope not explicit: JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5, ACCEPTABLE)\n  → NOT a CRITICAL_ERROR just because it's false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nConsider a $2025\\times2025$ grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of difference sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile.\n\nDetermine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nAll rows are numbered \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) from top to bottom and all columns are numbered \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) from left to right.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, there exists a permutation  \n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) let  \n\\[\nr_j=\\sigma^{-1}(j)\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nbe the row that contains the hole in column \\(j\\).  \nThus the hole in column \\(j\\) is the square \\((r_j,j)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Upper and lower regions  \n\nDefine the **upper region**  \n\\[\nA=\\bigcup_{j=1}^{n}\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le r_j-1\\},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nand the **lower region**  \n\\[\nB=\\bigcup_{j=1}^{n}\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nThe squares of the board are partitioned as  \n\\[\n\\{\\text{all unit squares}\\}=U\\;\\cup\\;A\\;\\cup\\;B .\n\\]\n\nFor each column set  \n\\[\nh_j:=r_j-1\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\ell_j:=n-r_j .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nThen \\(|A|=\\sum_{j=1}^{n}h_j\\) and \\(|B|=\\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\ell_j\\).  \nSince \\(\\{r_1,\\dots ,r_n\\}\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), the multisets \\(\\{h_1,\\dots ,h_n\\}\\) and \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) are both permutations of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Rectangles cannot cross a hole  \n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> Let \\(R\\) be a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines and which contains no hole. Then \\(R\\subseteq A\\) or \\(R\\subseteq B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the column interval of \\(R\\) as \\([a,b]\\) and its row interval as \\([c,d]\\) (both inclusive).  \nFor any column \\(j\\in[a,b]\\) the hole lies at row \\(r_j\\).  \nSince \\(R\\) does not contain \\((r_j,j)\\), either \\(d\\le r_j-1\\) (the rectangle stays entirely above the hole in column \\(j\\)) or \\(c\\ge r_j+1\\) (the rectangle stays entirely below the hole in column \\(j\\)).  \nIf there existed columns \\(j_1,j_2\\in[a,b]\\) with \\(d\\le r_{j_1}-1\\) and \\(c\\ge r_{j_2}+1\\), then the interval \\([c,d]\\) would be non‑empty and would contain an integer \\(t\\) with \\(r_{j_2}<t<r_{j_1}\\).  \nBecause the values \\(\\{r_j\\}\\) form a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), there is a column \\(j\\) with \\(r_j=t\\); then \\((t,j)\\in R\\), contradicting the hypothesis.  \nHence the same inequality holds for all columns in \\([a,b]\\), and \\(R\\) is either a subset of \\(A\\) or a subset of \\(B\\). ∎\n\nConsequently a tiling of the board splits into a tiling of \\(A\\) and a tiling of \\(B\\).  \nLet \\(N_A\\) (resp. \\(N_B\\)) be the number of rectangles used for \\(A\\) (resp. \\(B\\)).  \nThe total number of tiles is  \n\\[\nN=N_A+N_B .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for a histogram  \n\nConsider a histogram \\(H\\) consisting of columns of heights \\((c_1,\\dots ,c_n)\\) where \\(\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_n\\}\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there is a unique column, say column \\(p(i)\\), with height \\(c_{p(i)}=i\\).  \nThe cell \\((i,p(i))\\) is the **topmost** cell of column \\(p(i)\\).\n\n> **Lemma 2.**  \n> Any partition of such a histogram into axis‑aligned rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\mathcal R\\) be a family of rectangles that partitions the histogram.  \nFix \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and consider the topmost cell \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nSince column \\(p(i)\\) contains no cell above row \\(i\\), any rectangle of \\(\\mathcal R\\) that contains \\((i,p(i))\\) must have its top side on row \\(i\\).  \nA rectangle has a single top side, therefore it can contain at most one of the cells \\((i,p(i))\\) for different values of \\(i\\).  \nThus distinct values of \\(i\\) require distinct rectangles, and at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary. ∎\n\nApplying Lemma 2 to the upper region \\(A\\) (whose column heights are \\((h_1,\\dots ,h_n)\\)) yields  \n\\[\nN_A\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\nApplying Lemma 2 to the lower region \\(B\\) (whose column heights are \\((\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n)\\)) yields  \n\\[\nN_B\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Global lower bound  \n\nFrom (6), (7) and (8) we obtain  \n\\[\nN\\;=\\;N_A+N_B\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)\\;=\\;2(n-1).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus **any** placement of tiles satisfying the problem’s condition uses at least \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  A construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\), \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).\n\n*Upper region.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile.  \nThe family \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles and covers exactly the squares of the upper region.\n\n*Lower region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich is an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) tile.  \nThe family \\(\\{V_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles and covers exactly the squares of the lower region.\n\nThe two families are disjoint from each other, they together cover every unit square except the diagonal, and each row and each column contains precisely one uncovered square (the diagonal one).  \nThe total number of tiles used is  \n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nEquation (9) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles, while the construction in Section 6 shows that \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2(n-1)\\).  \n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\