f “left” and “right’’ are interchanged).  \nTherefore for **every** index \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) we obtain:\n\n* there exists at least one rectangle whose bottommost row is \\(i\\);\n* there exists at least one rectangle whose topmost row is \\(i+1\\).\n\nA rectangle possesses a unique bottommost row and a unique topmost row.  \nHence the collection of all rectangles in any tiling must contain at least\n\n* one distinct rectangle for each bottommost row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) (giving \\(n-1\\) rectangles), and\n* one distinct rectangle for each topmost row \\(i+1=2,\\dots ,n\\) (giving another \\(n-1\\) rectangles).\n\nThese two families are disjoint, because a rectangle cannot simultaneously have bottommost row \\(i\\) and topmost row \\(i+1\\) with \\(i< i+1\\).  \nConsequently any tiling of \\(L\\cup R\\) uses at least\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling attaining the bound\n\nChoose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\) (the uncovered squares lie on the main diagonal).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) define\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nT_i^{\\text{L}} &:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\}\\qquad (i=2,\\dots ,n),\\\\[2mm]\nT_i^{\\text{R}} &:=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\}\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach set \\(T_i^{\\text{L}}\\) (resp. \\(T_i^{\\text{R}}\\)) is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) (resp. \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines.  \nThe families \\(\\{T_i^{\\text{L}}\\}_{i=2}^{n}\\) and \\(\\{T_i^{\\text{R}}\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, because they occupy disjoint column intervals.  \n\nEvery cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<i\\) belongs to \\(T_i^{\\text{L}}\\); every cell with \\(j>i\\) belongs to \\(T_i^{\\text{R}}\\).  \nThus the union of all these rectangles is exactly \\(L\\cup R\\), i.e. the whole board except the diagonal uncovered squares.  \n\nThe number of tiles used is\n\\[\n|\\,\\{T_i^{\\text{L}}\\}\\,|+|