quares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other unit squares must be covered by tiles.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  A lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nConsider two consecutive rows \\(i\\) and \\(i+1\\) \\((1\\le i\\le n-1)\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\na=p(i),\\qquad b=p(i+1).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p\\) is a permutation, \\(a\\neq b\\); either \\(a<b\\) or \\(a>b\\).  \nThe argument is symmetric, so assume \\(a<b\\) (the case \\(a>b\\) is analogous).\n\n*Cell \\((i,b)\\).*  \nThe square \\((i+1,b)\\) belongs to \\(U\\) (it is the uncovered square of row \\(i+1\\)).  \nHence \\((i,b)\\) is a covered square and must belong to some tile.  \nIf a rectangle contained both rows \\(i\\) and \\(i+1\\), then it would also contain column \\(b\\) and therefore the uncovered square \\((i+1,b)\\), which is impossible.  \nConsequently any rectangle that contains \\((i,b)\\) cannot contain row \\(i+1\\); its **bottommost row** is forced to be exactly \\(i\\).\n\n*Cell \\((i+1,a)\\).*  \nAnalogously, \\((i,a)\\) is uncovered, so \\((i+1,a)\\) must be covered.  \nA rectangle containing \\((i+1,a)\\) cannot contain row \\(i\\); thus its **topmost row** is exactly \\(i+1\\).\n\nTherefore for each index \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) we obtain:\n\n* there exists at least one rectangle whose bottommost row is \\(i\\);\n* there exists at least one rectangle whose topmost row is \\(i+1\\).\n\nA rectangle possesses a unique bottommost row and a unique topmost row.  \nA rectangle whose bottommost row is \\(i\\) cannot have topmost row \\(i+1\\) (otherwise it would contain both rows \\(i\\) and \\(i+1\\), contradicting the observation above).  \nHence the two families of rectangles are disjoint.  \n\nThus any tiling of the board must contain at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) tile covering the cells to the left of the diagonal.\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) tile covering the cells to the right of the diagonal.\n\nThe families \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=2}^{n}\\) and \\(\\{R_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy disjoint column intervals in each row.  \nEvery non‑diagonal cell \\((i,j)\\) satisfies either \\(j<i\\) (hence belongs to \\(L_i\\)) or \\(j>i\\) (hence belongs to \\(R_i\\)).  \nThus the union of all these rectangles is exactly the set of all covered squares, i.e. the whole board minus the diagonal.\n\nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{L_i\\}\\,|+|\\,\\{R_i\\}\\,|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Minimality  \n\nSection 2 proved that any admissible tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nSection 3 exhibited a tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the lower bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit verification for p=3 provided ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The solution should explicitly state \"since p>3 is prime, it's not divisible by 3, so p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\" for complete rigor. The claim is mathematically correct and the reasoning is sound, but lacks an explicit justification step. This is a presentation issue, not a mathematical error.\n\n**WRONG Classification (don't do this):**\n*   ~~**Location:** \"p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"~~\n    *   ~~**Issue:** Critical Error - This claim needs proof.~~ ❌ WRONG - This would be hypercritical; the claim is a standard fact about primes and the overall logic is valid.\n\n---\n\n**Example 2: Critical Error (truly invalid)**\n*This example shows a fundamental mathematical error.*\n\nProblem: \"Prove that √2 is irrational.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"I tried to express √2 as a fraction p/q for many different integers p and q, but I couldn't find any that work. After testing hundreds of fractions, I conclude that √2 cannot be expressed as a fraction. Therefore √2 is irrational.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: √2 is irrational ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check reasoning method: \"I tried many cases and failed\" ✗ INVALID (falls under EXCEPTION)\n3. Decision: Invalid reasoning → Classify as **Critical Error**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"I tried to express √2 as a fraction p/q for many different integers p and q, but I couldn't find any\"\n    *   **Issue:** Critical Error - Failure to find a counterexample is not a proof of a universal statement. The solution provides no rigorous argument (no proof by contradiction, no algebraic reasoning, no number theory). This falls under the IMPORTANT EXCEPTION in the decision rule: complete