ownwards. In the solution, they talk about \"bottommost row\" as the largest row index (lowest on the grid). Let's check: They say \"If a rectangle contained both rows i and i+1, then it would also contain column b and therefore the uncovered square (i+1,b)\". So they consider rows i and i+1 as adjacent rows. They then say \"any rectangle that contains (i,b) cannot contain row i+1; its bottommost row is forced to be exactly i.\" That suggests they consider bottommost row as the largest row index in the rectangle (i.e., the rectangle's lower side). Since row i+1 is below row i (if rows increase downward), then the rectangle cannot extend below i, so its bottommost row is i. So they treat bottommost row as the maximal row index. That is consistent: If rectangle cannot contain row i+1 (i.e., cannot go below i), then the bottommost row (the lowest row included) must be i. So the rectangle could extend upward (to rows less than i), but that does not affect bottommost row. So bottommost row being i is correct: The rectangle's lowest row is i; it can extend upward, but the bottommost row is i. So the statement is correct.\n\nSimilarly, for rectangle covering (i+1,a), it cannot contain row i (above), so its topmost row (the smallest row index) is i+1. That's correct.\n\nThus the lower bound argument holds.\n\nPotential justification gap: The solution does not explicitly state that the rectangle covering (i,b) exists; but it's implicit because each covered cell belongs to some rectangle (tile). So existence is guaranteed. That's fine.\n\nPotential minor gap: The solution uses \"disjoint families of rectangles\" and \"cannot have topmost row i+1 and bottommost row i simultaneously\". That's correct.\n\nThus no critical errors.\n\nNow we need to check if any construction claims are missing concrete details. The solution provides explicit definitions of L_i and R_i sets. That's concrete. So Level 2 passes.\n\nNow we need to check if any claims are false. The lower bound argumen