ver, they did say \"must belong to some tile\". Actually they said \"must belong to some tile\". Let's check: \"Cell (i,b). The square (i+1,b) belongs to U ... Hence (i,b) is a covered square and must belong to some tile.\" Yes they said that. So existence is given.\n\n- They also claim \"Thus any rectangle that contains (i,b) cannot contain row i+1; its bottommost row is forced to be exactly i.\" That's fine.\n\n- Then they claim \"Similarly for (i+1,a) ... topmost row is exactly i+1.\" Fine.\n\n- Then they claim \"there exists at least one rectangle whose bottommost row is i; there exists at least one rectangle whose topmost row is i+1.\" That's a direct conclusion from the existence of those rectangles.\n\n- Then they claim \"A rectangle possesses a unique bottommost row and a unique topmost row. A rectangle whose bottommost row is i cannot have topmost row i+1 (otherwise it would contain both rows i and i+1, contradicting the observation above).\" That's correct.\n\n- So lower bound proof is complete.\n\n- Construction: They define L_i and R_i sets. They claim families are pairwise disjoint because they occupy disjoint column intervals in each row. That's correct.\n\n- They claim \"Every non‑diagonal cell (i,j) satisfies either j<i (hence belongs to L_i) or j>i (hence belongs to R_i).\" That's correct.\n\n- They count tiles: (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2. That's correct.\n\n- They compute answer: 4048.\n\nThus no justification gaps that are significant. Possibly the only minor gap is that they didn't explicitly state that L_i and R_i are rectangles (tiles) but they are defined as sets of cells forming a contiguous horizontal segment, which is a rectangle. That's clear.\n\nThus Level 3: No issues, or maybe \"Justification Gap: none\". So we can list \"None\" or \"No issues\".\n\nThus final JSON:\n\n{\n  \"Level 1 Result\": \"CORRECT (answer 4048)\",\n  \"Level 2 Result\": \"VALID\",\n  \"Level 3 Result\": \"No presentation issues (no justification gaps or critical errors)\"