j)\\) lies in the same row interval \\([a,b]\\) and column interval \\([c,d]\\) as \\(R\\).  \nThus \\((r_j,\\;j)\\) would be covered by \\(R\\), contradicting the requirement that tiles avoid uncovered squares.  \n\nHence **no** rectangle avoiding uncovered squares can contain two distinct cells of \\(U\\).  \nAn identical argument shows that no such rectangle can contain two distinct cells of \\(L\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A rectangle cannot contain an above‑cell and a below‑cell  \n\nIf a rectangle \\(R\\) contains a cell \\((r_j-1,j)\\in U\\), then its row interval must be contained in \\([1,\\,r_j-1]\\); otherwise it would also contain row \\(r_j\\) and hence the uncovered square \\((r_j,j)\\).  \nIf \\(R\\) contains a cell \\((r_j+1,j)\\in L\\), its row interval must be contained in \\([r_j+1,\\,n]\\).  \nThese two intervals are disjoint, so a single rectangle cannot contain both an above‑cell and a below‑cell from the same column, and therefore cannot contain one from \\(U\\) and one from \\(L\\) simultaneously.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nBecause each rectangle can cover **at most one** cell of \\(U\\) (Section 2) and the cells of \\(U\\) are pairwise distinct, at least \\(|U|=n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all cells of \\(U\\).  \nSimilarly, at least \\(|L|=n-1\\) rectangles are needed for the cells of \\(L\\).  \nSince a rectangle cannot serve both purposes (Section 3), the total number of rectangles in any admissible tiling satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the **identity permutation** \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThus the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place a **vertical tile** covering  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\, (i,j)\\;:\\;1\\le i\\le j-1 \\,\\},\n\\]\n\nthe squares strictly above the diagonal in column \\(j\\).  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place a vertical tile covering  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\, (i,j)\\;:\\;j+1\\le i\\le n \\,\\},\n\\]\n\nthe squares strictly below the diagonal in column \\(j\\).  \n\nEach tile is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines, the tiles are pairwise disjoint, and together they cover every unit square except the diagonal ones.  \nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality  \n\nThe lower bound (2) and the explicit construction (3) coincide, therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  \n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
