nL=\\{(r_j+1,\\;j)\\mid 1\\le r_j\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe top‑most uncovered square has \\(r_j=1\\) for exactly one column, so it contributes no element to \\(U\\); similarly the bottom‑most uncovered square has \\(r_j=n\\) for exactly one column, so it contributes no element to \\(L\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n|U|=|L|=n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll squares of the board that are not uncovered must be covered by tiles; in particular every cell of \\(U\\cup L\\) must belong to some tile.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.** No axis‑aligned rectangle that contains no uncovered square can contain two distinct cells of \\(U\\).\n\n*Proof.* Suppose a rectangle \\(R\\) contains \\((r_j-1,j)\\) and \\((r_k-1,k)\\) with \\(j\\neq k\\).  \nWithout loss assume \\(r_j\\le r_k\\).  \nLet the row interval of \\(R\\) be \\([a,b]\\) (integers) and its column interval be \\([c,d]\\).  \nSince \\((r_j-1,j)\\in R\\), we have \\(a\\le r_j-1\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le j\\le d\\); similarly \\(a\\le r_k-1\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le k\\le d\\).  \nBecause \\(r_j\\le r_k\\), we have \\(b\\ge r_k-1\\ge r_j\\). Thus row \\(r_j\\) lies in \\([a,b]\\). Together with column \\(j\\in[c,d]\\), the uncovered square \\((r_j,j)\\) belongs to \\(R\\), contradicting the hypothesis. ∎\n\n**Lemma 2.** No such rectangle can contain two distinct cells of \\(L\\). The proof is identical to Lemma 1 after shifting indices.\n\n**Lemma 3.** No such rectangle can contain simultaneously a cell of \\(U\\) and a cell of \\(L\\).\n\n*Proof.* Assume \\(R\\) contains \\((r_j-1,j)\\in U\\) and \\((r_k+1,k)\\in L\\).  \nThen \\(a\\le r_j-1\\le b\\) and \\(a\\le r_k+1\\le b\\).  \n\nIf \\(r_j-1\\le r_k+1\\), then \\(b\\ge r_k+1\\ge r_j\\), so row \\(r_j\\) lies in \\([a,b]\\) and column \\(j\\in[c,d]\\); thus \\((r_j,j)\\) is in \\(R\\), a contradiction.  \n\nIf \\(r_k+1\\le r_j-1\\), then \\(a\\le r_k+1\\le r_j-1\\), so \\(a\\le r_k\\le b\\); together with column \\(k\\) we obtain the uncovered square \\((r_k,k)\\) inside \\(R\\), again a contradiction. ∎\n\nFrom Lemmas 1–3 we conclude that any rectangle avoiding all uncovered squares can contain **at most one** element of \\(U\\cup L\\).  \n\nSince every element of \\(U\\cup L\\) must be covered, the number of tiles in any admissible tiling satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;|U|+|L| = (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Construction achieving the bound.**  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place a vertical tile covering the set  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{up}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. all squares strictly above the diagonal in column \\(j\\).  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place a vertical tile covering  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{down}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. all squares strictly below the diagonal in column \\(j\\).\n\nEach \\(T^{\\text{up}}_j\\) and each \\(T^{\\text{down}}_j\\) is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines.  \nThe families \\(\\{T^{\\text{up}}_j\\}\\) and \\(\\{T^{\\text{down}}_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, and together they cover every non‑diagonal square exactly once.  \n\nThe total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Minimality.**  \nInequality (2) gives a universal lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while construction (3) achieves this bound. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nThus the required minimum number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Self-Correction Instruction ###**  \n\nBefore finalizing, verify that:\n\n1. The permutation model correctly captures the condition “exactly one uncovered square per row and column”.\n2. Sets \\(U\\) and \\(L\\) indeed contain all squares adjacent (above or below) to uncovered squares and have size \\(n-1\\) each.\n3. Lemmas 1–3 are proved without gaps; each case analysis shows that a rectangle containing two forced cells would necessarily contain an uncovered square.\n4. The lower bound argument correctly uses the fact that every forced cell must be covered and each tile can cover at most one forced cell.\n5. The construction with the identity permutation uses disjoint vertical rectangles, covers all non‑diagonal squares, and uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n6. The final answer is presented in \\(\\boxed{}\\) and all mathematics is enclosed in TeX delimiters.  \n\nAfter confirming these points, the solution is complete and rigorous.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit verification for p=3 provided ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The solution should explicitly state \"since p>3 is prime, it's not divisible by 3, so p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\" for complete rigor. The claim is mathematically correct and the reasoning is sound, but lacks an explicit justification step. This is a presentation issue, not a mathematical error.\n\n**WRONG Classification (don't do this):**\n*   ~~**Location:** \"p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"~~\n    *   ~~**Issue:**