uare in that row.  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map \\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a permutation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Geometry of a tile\n\nConsider a rectangular tile \\(T\\).  \nLet its set of rows be an interval \\([a,b]\\) and its set of columns be an interval \\([c,d]\\) (both inclusive).  \nFor any row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) the uncovered square in that row is \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nSince a tile may not contain an uncovered square, we must have either  \n\n\\[\nd<p(i)\\qquad\\text{(the whole tile lies left of the hole in row }i\\text{)} \n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nor  \n\n\\[\nc>p(i)\\qquad\\text{(the whole tile lies right of the hole in row }i\\text{)} .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus every tile is of **one** of the two types:\n\n* **left‑type** – it satisfies (1) for all its rows; it covers only squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<p(i)\\);\n* **right‑type** – it satisfies (2) for all its rows; it covers only squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j>p(i)\\).\n\nConsequently the set of all left‑type tiles must cover the whole *left region*\n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<p(i)\\},\n\\]\n\nand the right‑type tiles must cover the *right region*\n\n\\[\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the left region\n\nFix a column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_j=\\{\\,i\\mid p(i)>j\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nthe set of rows whose left region contains a square in column \\(j\\).  \nBecause the permutation \\(p\\) takes each value \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) exactly once, there is a unique row\n\\(i_j\\) with \\(p(i_j)=n\\); that row belongs to every \\(S_j\\). Hence each \\(S_j\\) is non‑empty.\n\nA left‑type tile that covers a square in column \\(j\\) must have its row interval contained in a\n*contiguous* subset of \\(S_j\\) (otherwise it would contain a row \\(i\\notin S_j\\) for which\n\\(j\\ge p(i)\\) and condition (1) would fail).  \nTherefore **at least one** left‑type tile is needed for each column \\(j\\).  \nSince there are \\(n-1\\) columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Number of left‑type tiles }\\ge n-1.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the right region\n\nThe right region is symmetric.  \nFor a column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_j=\\{\\,i\\mid p(i)<j\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nExactly the same argument as in §2 shows that each column \\(j\\) of the right region\nrequires at least one right‑type tile.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Number of right‑type tiles }\\ge n-1.}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Global lower bound\n\nAdding (3) and (4) we obtain for any admissible tiling\n\n\\[\n\\text{total number of tiles }\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the **identity permutation** \\(p(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the diagonal\n\\((i,i)\\)).  \n\n*Left region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) the set \\(S_j=\\{i\\mid i>j\\}=\n\\{j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a single interval.  \nCover it by the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nL_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has width 1 and height \\(n-j\\).  \nThese are \\(n-1\\) disjoint left‑type tiles.\n\n*Right region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) the set of rows with a right‑region square in\ncolumn \\(j\\) is \\(\\{i\\mid i<j\\}=\\{1,\\dots ,j-1\\}\\).  \nCover it by the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nR_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\},\n\\]\n\nagain a width‑1 rectangle.  \nThese are another \\(n-1\\) disjoint right‑type tiles.\n\nAll tiles are axis‑aligned rectangles, they are pairwise disjoint, and together they cover\nevery unit square except the \\(n\\) diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).  Consequently each row and\neach column contains exactly one uncovered square, namely its diagonal entry.\n\nThus we have exhibited a tiling with exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion for \\(n=2025\\)\n\nFrom (5) the number of tiles can never be smaller than \\(2(2025-1)=4048\\); the construction\nabove shows that \\(4048\\) tiles are sufficient.  Hence the minimum possible number of\ntiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
