vering a right\u2011type square in column $j$ has leftmost column exactly $j$.\n\nBecause a tile cannot contain both a left\u2011type and a right\u2011type square in the same column, the two cases are mutually exclusive.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nFor each column $j$ with $2\\le j\\le n-1$ both sets $L_j$ and $R_j$ are non\u2011empty by (3).  \nHence column $j$ contains at least one left\u2011type square and at least one right\u2011type square.  \nBy the discussion above:\n\n* there must exist a tile whose **rightmost column** is $j$ (to cover the left\u2011type squares of column $j$);\n* there must exist a (different) tile whose **leftmost column** is $j$ (to cover the right\u2011type squares of column $j$).\n\nThus each column $j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}$ forces **two distinct tiles**.\n\nFor $j=1$ the set $R_1$ is empty, so only left\u2011type squares occur; consequently at least one tile with rightmost column $1$ is required.  \nFor $j=n$ the set $L_n$ is empty, so at least one tile with leftmost column $n$ is required.\n\nSumming all required tiles we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\text{total number of tiles}\\;\\ge\\;2\\,(n-2)+2\\;=\\;2(n-1).\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore any admissible placement uses at least $2(n-1)$ tiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation $p(i)=i$; the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells $(i,i)$, $1\\le i\\le n$.\n\n*Left region.*  \nFor each $j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}$ define  \n\n\\[\nL_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n$L_j$ is a vertical rectangle of width\u202f1 and height $n-j$, its rightmost column is $j$, and it covers exactly the left\u2011region squares of column $j$.\n\n*Right region.*  \nFor each $j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}$ define  \n\n\\[\nR_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n$R_j$ is a vertical rectangle of width\u202f1 and height $j-1$, its leftmost column is $j$, and it covers exactly the right\u2011region squares of column $j$.\n\nThe families $\\{L_j\\}$ and $\\{R_j\\}$ are pairwise disjoint; moreover $L_j\\cap R_k=\\varnothing$ for all $j,k$ because $L_j$ lies strictly below the diagonal while $R_k$ lies strictly above it.  \nTogether they cover every unit square except the $n$ diagonal squares, so each row and each column contains precisely one uncovered square (its diagonal entry).\n\nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{L_j\\}\\,|+|\\,\\{R_j\\}\\,|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n\\]\n\nThus the bound (4) is tight.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Final answer  \n\nFor $n=2025$ the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2(2025-1)=4048.\n\\]  \n\nHence the answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing, verify that:\n\n* The \u201cPermutation of holes\u201d step correctly models the condition that each row and column has exactly one uncovered square.\n* The classification of a tile into left\u2011type or right\u2011type is proved rigorously via conditions (1) and (2).\n* The column\u2011wise argument shows that any tile covering a left\u2011type (resp. right\u2011type) square in column $j$ must have rightmost column $j$ (resp. leftmost column $j$), and that a tile cannot cover both types in the same column.\n* The counting argument in Section\u202f3 correctly yields the lower bound $2(n-1)$ for any permutation $p$.\n* The construction in Section\u202f4 indeed uses $2(n-1)$ disjoint rectangles and leaves exactly one uncovered square per row and column.\n* All mathematical expressions are enclosed in TeX delimiters.  \n\nAfter confirming these points, the solution is complete and ready for submission."
