                "reasoning_content": "We have a problem: a 2025x2025 grid of unit squares. Matilda places rectangular tiles (axis-aligned rectangles with sides on grid lines) covering squares, each unit square covered at most once. She wants each row and each column to have exactly one unit square not covered by any tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles needed.\n\nThe solution provided: defines n=2025, p(i) = column of uncovered square in row i. Since each column also has exactly one uncovered square, p is a permutation.\n\nThen defines left-type and right-type squares relative to a tile: a tile covering squares left of the hole (i.e., column less than p(i) for each row in tile) or right of the hole (col > p(i)). Then uses column-wise decomposition: define L_j = rows where p(i)>j (i.e., holes to the right of column j), and R_j = rows where p(i)<j (holes to left). Then counts sizes: |L_j| = n - j, |R_j| = j - 1.\n\nThen argues that any tile covering a left-type square in column j must have rightmost column exactly j, and any tile covering a right-type square in column j must have leftmost column exactly j. So each column j (2 ≤ j ≤ n-1) has both left and right types, thus requires at least two distinct tiles: one with rightmost column j, one with leftmost column j. For j=1, only left-type squares, need tile with rightmost column 1. For j=n, only right-type squares, need tile with leftmost column n. So total tiles ≥ 2(n-2)+2 = 2(n-1). So lower bound is 2(n-1).\n\nThen construction: choose identity permutation p(i)=i (holes on diagonal). Then left region: for each j from 1 to n-1, define L_j = {(i,j) | i=j+1,...,n} which is a vertical rectangle of width 1, height n-j, rightmost column j. Right region: for each j from 2 to n, define R_j = {(i,j) | i=1,...,j-1} vertical rectangle width 1, height j-1, leftmost column j. These are disjoint, cover all squares except diagonal. So total tiles = (n-1)+(n-1) = 2(n-1). So bound tight.\n\nThus answer: 2(2025-1) = 4048.\n\nWe need to evaluate the solution according to the hierarchical decision tree.\n\nFirst, Level 1: answer correctness. The answer is 4048. Is that correct? Let's verify quickly: The solution gives a lower bound of 2(n-1) and a construction achieving that bound, so answer is 2(n-1). For n=2025, that's 4048. Is that indeed minimal? Let's think: Could we possibly do with fewer tiles? The solution's lower bound argument seems plausible. Let's check for any potential oversight. The lower bound argument is based on each column j (2 ≤ j ≤ n-1) requiring two distinct tiles: one covering left-type squares (rightmost column j) and one covering right-type squares (leftmost column j). The argument that each column j must have at least one left-type square and at least one right-type square is based on the permutation property: for each column j, there are rows i with p(i) > j (holes to the right) and rows i with p(i) < j (holes to left). Indeed, for any j between 2 and n-1, there are both rows where p(i) > j and rows where p(i) < j, because the permutation includes values less than j and greater than j. So L_j and R_j are non-empty. So column j has both left-type squares (those squares left of the hole in rows where hole is to the right) and right-type squares (those squares right of the hole in rows where hole is to the left). So each column j has at least one left-type and one right-type square.\n\nNow the argument that any tile covering a left-type square in column j must have rightmost column exactly j: Let's examine. Suppose tile T covers a left-type square (i,j) where p(i) > j (hole to the right). For tile T to not contain the hole, for each row i' in its row interval [a,b], we must have either all squares covered are left of the hole (i.e., tile's columns are ≤ d < p(i') for all i'), or all squares covered are right of the hole (c > p(i')). Since tile includes (i,j) which is left of the hole for row i, it must be left-type for all rows in the tile (since tile cannot have both left-type and right-type squares in same column). So for each row i' in [a,b], we have d < p(i'). Since p(i') > j for all i' in [a,b] (since they belong to L_j), we have d ≤ j? Actually we need d < p(i') for each i'. The maximum possible d that satisfies d < p(i') for all i' is the minimum of p(i') - 1 across i' in [a,b]. Since p(i') > j, the minimum p(i') could be j+1 (if some row has hole at column j+1). Then d ≤ j. But tile includes column j (since it covers (i,j)), so d ≥ j. So d = j. So rightmost column of tile is j. So argument holds.\n\nSimilarly for right-type squares: tile's leftmost column must be j.\n\nThus each column j (2 ≤ j ≤ n-1) requires at least two distinct tiles: one with rightmost column j (covering left-type squares) and one with leftmost column j (covering right-type squares). Could the same tile serve both for different columns? No, because tile's rightmost column is fixed, so a tile with rightmost column j cannot also have leftmost column k for another column? Wait, a tile could cover left-type squares in column j and also right-type squares in column j' for some j' > j? Let's examine: Suppose a tile has rightmost column j. It covers left-type squares in column j. Could it also cover right-type squares in column j'? For column j', the tile would need leftmost column = j' (if covering right-type squares). But tile's leftmost column is some c ≤ j (since its rightmost column is j). So it cannot have leftmost column j' > j. So cannot cover right-type squares in any column > j. Could it cover right-type squares in column j-1? For column j-1, to cover right-type squares, tile's leftmost column must be j-1. But tile's leftmost column is c ≤ j (maybe c = j-1?), but tile's rightmost column is j, so leftmost column could be j-1. So tile could be of width 2 covering columns j-1 and j. Could it cover right-type squares in column j-1? Let's see: For column j-1, right-type squares are those squares to the right of the hole in rows where p(i) < j-1. The tile covering columns j-1 and j includes column j-1 squares, but also column j squares. If tile's leftmost column is j-1, then for rows in its interval, we need c > p(i) for all rows (since it's covering right-type squares). So c = j-1 > p(i) for all rows i in tile. That would require p(i) ≤ j-2 for all rows in tile. But tile also covers column j squares. For those rows, column j is > p(i) as well (since p(i) ≤ j-2). So tile could be right-type for column j-1. But then for column j, tile would be covering squares left of the hole? Wait, tile includes column j squares. For column j, left-type squares are those left of the hole (p(i) > j). The tile's rightmost column is j, so for left-type squares, we need d = j < p(i). So p(i) > j for all rows in tile. But we just said p(i) ≤ j-2 for all rows (to be right-type for column j-1). That's contradictory. So tile cannot simultaneously be left-type for column j and right-type for column j-1. So indeed each column j requires distinct tile for left-type and distinct tile for right-type.\n\nThus lower bound stands.\n\nConstruction: identity permutation yields diagonal holes. Then left region: for each column j, vertical rectangle covering rows below diagonal (i > j) in column j. That's a vertical strip of width 1, height n-j. Right region: for each column j, vertical rectangle covering rows above diagonal (i < j) in column j, width 1, height j-1. These are disjoint and cover all squares except diagonal. So total tiles = (n-1)+(n-1) = 2(n-1). So construction matches lower bound.\n\nThus answer is correct.\n\nNow we need to check Level 2: Reasoning validity. The solution uses valid mathematical principles: permutation modeling, case analysis, counting argument, construction. The solution also uses a classification of tile types and column-wise decomposition. All are valid methods. It also uses a constructive proof for lower bound and explicit construction for upper bound. So reasoning is valid.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation quality. Are there any justification gaps or critical errors? Let's examine the solution for any missing details or ambiguous statements.\n\nThe solution defines left-type and right-type squares and argues that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column. The reasoning is that if a tile covered both types, then the hole would be inside the tile. This is correct.\n\nThe solution's argument that any tile covering a left-type square in column j must have rightmost column exactly j: they argue that [a,b] subset of L_j, and d must be the largest column that can be used while staying left of all holes in [a,b]; thus d=j. This is plausible but they assume that there exists a row i in [a,b] with p(i) = j+1 (i.e., the minimal p(i) among rows in L_j is j+1). Actually, they need to argue that the minimal p(i) among rows in L_j is at least j+1, and that there exists a row with p(i) = j+1. But is that always true? For a given column j, L_j = {i | p(i) > j}. The minimal p(i) among those rows could be > j+1, e.g., if the permutation p has no value equal to j+1. But since p is a permutation of {1,...,n}, there must be exactly one row i0 such that p(i0) = j+1. So that row belongs to L_j. So the minimal p(i) among L_j is indeed j+1. So d must be ≤ j (since d < p(i) for all i in [a,b]), but also tile includes column j, so d ≥ j. So d=j. So the argument is valid.\n\nSimilarly for right-type squares.\n\nThus the reasoning is valid.\n\nNow check for any missing details: The solution doesn't explicitly prove that the tile covering left-type squares in column j must have rightmost column exactly j, but they provide a reasoning. This is a justification gap? The argument is fairly complete; they mention that d must be the largest column that can be used while staying left of all holes in [a,b]; hence d=j. This is a bit informal but acceptable. The solution also doesn't explicitly prove that the tiles for left-type squares for each column j are distinct from those for right-type squares for each column j. They argue that each column j forces two distinct tiles because one tile's rightmost column is j and the other tile's leftmost column is j, and a tile cannot have both rightmost and leftmost column equal to j unless it is a single column tile (width 1). Could a single column tile serve both roles? Let's examine: If a tile has width 1 (c = d = j), then it could be both left-type and right-type? Let's see: If tile covers column j only, then for rows i in tile, the tile's column j must be either left of the hole (j < p(i)) or right of the hole (j > p(i)). It cannot be both for the same row. But could the tile cover left-type squares for some rows and right-type squares for other rows? The tile's column is j, so for rows where p(i) > j, tile is left-type; for rows where p(i) < j, tile is right-type. So a single column tile could contain both left-type squares (for rows where hole is to the right) and right-type squares (for rows where hole is to the left). However, the solution earlier argued that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column because that would force the hole to be within the tile. Let's examine that claim: Suppose a tile covers column j only (c = d = j). For a row i where p(i) > j, tile includes column j which is left of the hole (since j < p(i)). For a row i' where p(i') < j, tile includes column j which is right of the hole (since j > p(i')). So the tile includes squares left of the hole for some rows and right of the hole for others. Does that cause the tile to contain the hole? The hole is at (i,p(i)) for each row i. The tile includes only column j, so the hole (i,p(i)) is not in the tile unless p(i) = j. But for rows where p(i) > j, p(i) != j; for rows where p(i) < j, also not j. So the tile does not contain any hole. So the tile can contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column, as long as it does not include the hole column. So the claim that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column is false. Wait, the solution says: \"A tile cannot contain both a left‑type square and a right‑type square in the same column, because that would force the hole $(i,p(i))$ to belong to $[c,d]$.\" Let's examine that statement. If a tile covers column j only, then for rows i where p(i) > j, tile includes left-type squares; for rows i where p(i) < j, tile includes right-type squares. The hole column p(i) is not j for those rows, so the tile does not contain the hole. So the claim is false. However, the solution's later argument about column j requiring two distinct tiles may still hold because they require a tile covering left-type squares in column j must have rightmost column j, and a tile covering right-type squares in column j must have leftmost column j. Could a single tile with both leftmost and rightmost column j (i.e., width 1) serve both roles? Let's examine: If a tile has width 1 (c = d = j), then its leftmost column is j and its rightmost column is also j. So it could be considered both a tile with leftmost column j (covering right-type squares) and a tile with rightmost column j (covering left-type squares). So a single tile could potentially cover both left-type and right-type squares in column j, thus fulfilling both requirements. The solution's claim that the two tiles must be distinct may be invalid. However, the solution's lower bound argument says \"there must exist a tile whose rightmost column is j (to cover the left‑type squares of column j); there must exist a (different) tile whose leftmost column is j (to cover the right‑type squares of column j).\" They assert the tile must be different. But is that necessarily true? Could one tile have both leftmost and rightmost column j? That would be a tile of width 1. Does such a tile cover both left-type and right-type squares? Let's examine the definitions: left-type squares are those squares (i,j) where j < p(i) (hole to the right). Right-type squares are those squares (i,j) where j > p(i) (hole to the left). For a tile of width 1 at column j, it includes squares (i,j) for rows i in its row interval. For each such row i, either j < p(i) (left-type) or j > p(i) (right-type). So the tile includes both left-type and right-type squares across different rows. The solution's earlier claim that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column is false. However, they might be using a stricter definition: they might require that a tile covering left-type squares in column j must have all rows i in its interval satisfy p(i) > j. Similarly, for a tile covering right-type squares in column j, all rows i in its interval must satisfy p(i) < j. So a tile that includes both left-type and right-type squares would have rows i where p(i) > j and rows i where p(i) < j. That would violate the condition that the tile must be either left-type for all its rows or right-type for all its rows. Let's re-express: The definition of left-type tile: For each row i in [a,b], we have d < p(i). That is, the tile's rightmost column d is less than the hole column p(i). So for a tile covering column j, we have d=j. So left-type condition is j < p(i) for all rows i in tile. Right-type condition is c > p(i) for all rows i in tile, i.e., j > p(i) for all rows i in tile. So if a tile includes rows i where p(i) > j and rows i' where p(i') < j, then neither condition holds for all rows. So the tile would not be allowed to cover both left-type and right-type squares in the same column because the tile must satisfy either condition (1) or (2) for all rows i in its interval. Indeed, the definition of left-type tile is that the tile lies entirely left of the hole for each row it covers. That means for each row i in tile, the tile's columns are all less than p(i). Similarly, right-type tile lies entirely right of the hole for each row. So a tile cannot have some rows where it's left of the hole and some rows where it's right of the hole, because then the tile would intersect the hole for some row? Actually, the tile would be left of the hole for some rows and right of the hole for others, but still not contain any hole. However, the tile would not satisfy either condition (1) or (2) for all rows, because condition (1) requires d < p(i) for all i in [a,b]; condition (2) requires c > p(i) for all i in [a,b]. If we have rows i where p(i) > j and rows i' where p(i') < j, then for those rows, condition (1) fails for i' (since d=j is not less than p(i') which is < j) and condition (2) fails for i (since c=j is not greater than p(i) which is > j). So the tile does not satisfy either condition for all rows, thus the tile would be invalid according to the earlier definition. But is it allowed for a tile to have rows where both conditions hold? The solution's definition of left-type and right-type squares is based on the tile's location relative to the hole for each row. They claim that for a tile to not contain any hole, for each row i in its interval, either the tile is entirely left of the hole (i.e., d < p(i)) or entirely right of the hole (c > p(i)). However, they didn't require the same condition for all rows; they said \"For any row i in [a,b] the uncovered square in that row is (i,p(i)). Since T may not contain an uncovered square, we must have for every i in [a,b] either d < p(i) or c > p(i).\" Actually, they wrote: \"Since $T$ may not contain an uncovered square, we must have for every $i\\in[a,b]$ either  \n\n\\[\nd<p(i)\\qquad\\text{(the whole tile lies left of the hole in row }i\\text{)}\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nor  \n\n\\[\nc>p(i)\\qquad\\text{(the whole tile lies right of the hole in row }i\\text{)}.\\tag{2}\n\\]\"\n\nThus they allow for each row i, either condition (1) or (2) could hold, potentially varying by row. But then they later define left-type and right-type tiles as those where the tile satisfies (1) for all its rows (left-type) or (2) for all its rows (right-type). They say: \"Thus a tile can cover only squares of one of the two types in a given column: left-type – the tile satisfies (1) for all its rows; right-type – the tile satisfies (2) for all its rows.\" They claim \"A tile cannot contain both a left‑type square and a right‑type square in the same column, because that would force the hole $(i,p(i))$ to belong to $[c,d]$.\" However, as we reasoned, a tile could have rows where condition (1) holds and rows where condition (2) holds, but then the tile would contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column. Does that force the hole to belong to [c,d]? Let's examine: Suppose tile T covers rows i in [a,b] and columns [c,d]. For a row i where condition (1) holds, we have d < p(i). For a row i' where condition (2) holds, we have c > p(i'). Since c ≤ d (by definition of interval), we have c ≤ d. If there exists i such that d < p(i) and i' such that c > p(i'), then we have c > p(i') and d < p(i). Since c ≤ d, we get p(i') < c ≤ d < p(i). So p(i') < p(i). So the holes for those rows are on opposite sides of the tile's column interval. The tile does not contain any hole because for each row i, the tile's columns are either entirely left of the hole (if d < p(i)) or entirely right of the hole (if c > p(i')). So the tile does not contain any hole. So it's possible for a tile to have rows where condition (1) holds and rows where condition (2) holds, as long as the tile's column interval lies between the holes for those rows. But the tile's column interval is fixed; for any row i where p(i) ≤ c, condition (2) holds; for any row i where p(i) ≥ d+1, condition (1) holds. So if there are rows with holes on both sides of the tile, the tile can be placed in the \"gap\" between the holes. However, the tile's rows must be contiguous (since it's a rectangle). So the rows in the tile must be a contiguous interval [a,b] such that all holes for rows in [a,b] are either all > d (to the right) or all < c (to the left), or some are on both sides but then the tile would have to be placed between the smallest hole on the right side and the largest hole on the left side, which is impossible because the holes are distinct columns. Actually, let's analyze more concretely.\n\nSuppose we have a tile covering columns [c,d]. For each row i in the tile's row interval, the hole column p(i) must be either > d (hole to the right) or < c (hole to the left). If there is a row i1 with p(i1) > d and a row i2 with p(i2) < c, then we have p(i2) < c ≤ d < p(i1). So the holes are on opposite sides of the tile. That's okay; the tile does not contain any hole. So the tile can have rows where holes are on both sides, as long as the tile's column interval lies between the smallest hole column among rows where holes are on the right side and the largest hole column among rows where holes are on the left side. But if the tile's rows are contiguous, the holes for those rows may be interleaved. However, it's possible that the holes for rows in [a,b] are not monotonic, but the tile's columns are fixed. For the tile to be valid, all holes for rows in [a,b] must be either all > d or all < c, or else there would be rows where p(i) is between c and d inclusive, which would cause the tile to contain the hole. Actually, if there is a row i where p(i) is between c and d inclusive, then the tile would contain the hole, which is not allowed. So for each row i in [a,b], p(i) must be either > d or < c. It cannot be that for some rows p(i) > d and for others p(i) < c, because then the tile's column interval [c,d] would be between those holes, but that's fine: p(i) > d for some rows, p(i) < c for others. The tile does not contain any hole. So the tile can have rows where holes are on both sides. So the tile can contain both left-type squares (for rows where p(i) > d) and right-type squares (for rows where p(i) < c). So the claim that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column is false. However, the solution's later argument about lower bound uses the fact that each column j requires two distinct tiles: one with rightmost column j (covering left-type squares) and one with leftmost column j (covering right-type squares). Could a single tile with columns [c,d] where c ≤ j ≤ d be used to cover both left-type squares in column j (for rows with p(i) > j) and right-type squares in column j (for rows with p(i) < j)? Let's examine: If tile covers column j, then c ≤ j ≤ d. For rows where p(i) > j, we need d < p(i) for left-type condition, but d may be ≥ j, so we need d < p(i). That's okay if p(i) > d. For rows where p(i) < j, we need c > p(i) for right-type condition, which is okay if c > p(i). So if the tile's column interval [c,d] is such that c > max of p(i) for rows where p(i) < j, and d < min of p(i) for rows where p(i) > j, then the tile can contain both left-type and right-type squares in column j. However, the tile's columns must be contiguous and include j. So we need c ≤ j ≤ d. So we need c ≤ j ≤ d, c > p(i) for rows with p(i) < j, and d < p(i) for rows with p(i) > j. This is possible if there is a \"gap\" between the largest p(i) among rows with p(i) < j and the smallest p(i) among rows with p(i) > j, and that gap includes column j. Since p is a permutation, the set of p(i) values for rows in the tile is some subset of {1,...,n}. The largest p(i) less than j is at most j-1; the smallest p(i) greater than j is at least j+1. So there is at least one column (j) between them. So we could choose c = j, d = j, i.e., tile width 1 at column j. Then c > p(i) for rows with p(i) < j? Actually c = j > p(i) for rows where p(i) ≤ j-1, so condition holds. d = j < p(i) for rows where p(i) ≥ j+1, condition holds. So a width-1 tile at column j works for rows where holes are on both sides. So a tile of width 1 at column j can indeed contain both left-type and right-type squares in column j, as we argued earlier. So the solution's claim that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column is false. However, does this affect the lower bound argument? The lower bound argument uses the fact that each column j (2 ≤ j ≤ n-1) has both left-type and right-type squares, and thus requires two distinct tiles: one with rightmost column j (covering left-type squares) and one with leftmost column j (covering right-type squares). But as we just argued, a single width-1 tile at column j could cover both left-type and right-type squares in column j, satisfying both requirements with a single tile. However, does that tile also satisfy the requirement that it covers all left-type squares in column j? The left-type squares in column j are those squares (i,j) where p(i) > j. The width-1 tile at column j can cover any subset of rows in column j, but not necessarily all rows where p(i) > j. It can only cover a contiguous interval of rows (since tile is a rectangle). So to cover all left-type squares in column j, which are rows i ∈ L_j = {i | p(i) > j}, the tile would need to cover all those rows. But L_j may not be a contiguous interval. Indeed, L_j is the set of rows where the hole is to the right of column j. Since p is a permutation, the rows where p(i) > j could be scattered across the rows. For the identity permutation p(i) = i, L_j = {i > j}, which is a contiguous interval [j+1, n]. So for identity permutation, L_j is contiguous, and a single vertical tile covering column j and rows j+1 to n (i.e., the left region L_j) would cover all left-type squares in column j. Similarly, the right-type squares are rows i < j, which are contiguous [1, j-1]; a single vertical tile covering column j and rows 1 to j-1 would cover all right-type squares. But those are two separate tiles (one covering rows above the diagonal, one covering rows below). Could we combine them into a single tile covering rows 1 to n (i.e., whole column j)? That would include the diagonal square (i=j, p(i)=j), which is the uncovered square. That tile would contain the uncovered square, which is not allowed. So we cannot have a tile covering the entire column j because it would contain the uncovered square at (j,j). So we need at least two tiles for each column j: one covering rows above the diagonal (right-type squares) and one covering rows below the diagonal (left-type squares). However, the lower bound argument says each column j forces two distinct tiles, but they didn't consider the possibility that a tile could cover left-type squares for some rows and right-type squares for other rows, but not both for the same column because it would contain the uncovered square? Actually, if a tile covers column j and rows both above and below the diagonal, it would include the diagonal square (j,j) which is uncovered. So that tile is not allowed. So we need at least two tiles for each column j in the identity case. So the lower bound argument holds for identity permutation. But does it hold for any permutation? Let's examine a general permutation p. For a given column j, L_j = rows where p(i) > j, R_j = rows where p(i) < j. The uncovered squares are at positions (i,p(i)). For column j, there is exactly one row i0 such that p(i0) = j (the uncovered square in column j). So L_j and R_j are separated by that row i0: all rows i with p(i) > j could be above or below i0; similarly for p(i) < j. The sets L_j and R_j are not necessarily contiguous intervals. However, the tile covering left-type squares in column j must have rightmost column j and must be left of the holes for all rows it covers. It could cover some subset of rows from L_j, but not necessarily all of them. The lower bound argument says \"there must exist a tile whose rightmost column is j (to cover the left‑type squares of column j)\". It doesn't require covering all left-type squares, just at least one left-type square. Similarly, there must exist a tile whose leftmost column is j (to cover the right‑type squares). So each column j forces at least one tile of each type. However, could the same tile serve as both a tile with rightmost column j and leftmost column j? That would require the tile's leftmost column = rightmost column = j (width 1). So a width-1 tile could be both. But would it be able to cover at least one left-type square and at least one right-type square? Yes, if the tile's row interval includes at least one row from L_j and at least one row from R_j. But can a tile include rows from both L_j and R_j? As we argued, a tile can include rows where holes are on both sides, as long as the tile's column interval lies between the holes. For a width-1 tile at column j, the column interval is just j. For rows i in L_j (p(i) > j), condition (1) holds: d=j < p(i). For rows i in R_j (p(i) < j), condition (2) holds: c=j > p(i). So the tile can include rows from both L_j and R_j. However, does the tile's row interval need to be contiguous? Yes. So we need a contiguous interval of rows that includes at least one row from L_j and at least one row from R_j. Since L_j and R_j are separated by the row i0 where p(i0)=j, we could have a tile covering rows that include i0? No, because tile cannot include the uncovered square at (i0,j). So the tile's row interval cannot include i0. So the tile's rows must be either entirely above i0 or entirely below i0 (or possibly both if we skip i0, but row interval must be contiguous, so cannot skip i0). So a tile cannot include rows from both L_j and R_j because those rows are separated by i0 (the row where p(i)=j). Since the tile cannot include i0, the row interval cannot cross i0. Therefore, any tile can only include rows from either L_j or R_j, not both. So a width-1 tile cannot cover both left-type and right-type squares for column j. So the solution's claim that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column is indeed true under the additional constraint that the tile cannot contain the uncovered square (i0,j). Because to include rows from both L_j and R_j, the tile's row