eft-type and right-type squares in same column). So for each row i' in [a,b], we have d < p(i'). Since p(i') > j for all i' in [a,b] (since they belong to L_j), we have d ≤ j? Actually we need d < p(i') for each i'. The maximum possible d that satisfies d < p(i') for all i' is the minimum of p(i') - 1 across i' in [a,b]. Since p(i') > j, the minimum p(i') could be j+1 (if some row has hole at column j+1). Then d ≤ j. But tile includes column j (since it covers (i,j)), so d ≥ j. So d = j. So rightmost column of tile is j. So argument holds.\n\nSimilarly for right-type squares: tile's leftmost column must be j.\n\nThus each column j (2 ≤ j ≤ n-1) requires at least two distinct tiles: one with rightmost column j (covering left-type squares) and one with leftmost column j (covering right-type squares). Could the same tile serve both for different columns? No, because tile's rightmost column is fixed, so a tile with rightmost column j cannot also have leftmost column k for another column? Wait, a tile could cover left-type squares in column j and also right-type squares in column j' for some j' > j? Let's examine: Suppose a tile has rightmost column j. It covers left-type squares in column j. Could it also cover right-type squares in column j'? For column j', the tile would need leftmost column = j' (if covering right-type squares). But tile's leftmost column is some c ≤ j (since its rightmost column is j). So it cannot have leftmost column j' > j. So cannot cover right-type squares in any column > j. Could it cover right-type squares in column j-1? For column j-1, to cover right-type squares, tile's leftmost column must be j-1. But tile's leftmost column is c ≤ j (maybe c = j-1?), but tile's rightmost column is j, so leftmost column could be j-1. So tile could be of width 2 covering columns j-1 and j. Could it cover right-type squares in column j-1? Let's see: For column j-1, right-type squares are those squares to the right of the hole in rows where p(i) < j-1. The til