y. Are there any justification gaps or critical errors? Let's examine the solution for any missing details or ambiguous statements.\n\nThe solution defines left-type and right-type squares and argues that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column. The reasoning is that if a tile covered both types, then the hole would be inside the tile. This is correct.\n\nThe solution's argument that any tile covering a left-type square in column j must have rightmost column exactly j: they argue that [a,b] subset of L_j, and d must be the largest column that can be used while staying left of all holes in [a,b]; thus d=j. This is plausible but they assume that there exists a row i in [a,b] with p(i) = j+1 (i.e., the minimal p(i) among rows in L_j is j+1). Actually, they need to argue that the minimal p(i) among rows in L_j is at least j+1, and that there exists a row with p(i) = j+1. But is that always true? For a given column j, L_j = {i | p(i) > j}. The minimal p(i) among those rows could be > j+1, e.g., if the permutation p has no value equal to j+1. But since p is a permutation of {1,...,n}, there must be exactly one row i0 such that p(i0) = j+1. So that row belongs to L_j. So the minimal p(i) among L_j is indeed j+1. So d must be ≤ j (since d < p(i) for all i in [a,b]), but also tile includes column j, so d ≥ j. So d=j. So the argument is valid.\n\nSimilarly for right-type squares.\n\nThus the reasoning is valid.\n\nNow check for any missing details: The solution doesn't explicitly prove that the tile covering left-type squares in column j must have rightmost column exactly j, but they provide a reasoning. This is a justification gap? The argument is fairly complete; they mention that d must be the largest column that can be used while staying left of all holes in [a,b]; hence d=j. This is a bit informal but acceptable. The solution also doesn't explicitly prove that the tiles for left-type squares for each column j are distinct from those for right-type squares for each column j. They argue that each column j forces two distinct tiles because one tile's rightmost column is j and the other tile's leftmost column is j, and a tile cannot have both rightmost and leftmost column equal to j unless it is a single column tile (width 1). Could a single column tile serve both roles? Let's examine: If a tile has width 1 (c = d = j), then it could be both left-type and right-type? Let's see: If tile covers column j only, then for rows i in tile, the tile's column j must be either left of the hole (j < p(i)) or right of the hole (j > p(i)). It cannot be both for the same row. But could the tile cover left-type squares for some rows and right-type squares for other rows? The tile's column is j, so for rows where p(i) > j, tile is left-type; for rows where p(i) < j, tile is right-type. So a single column tile could contain both left-type squares (for rows where hole is to the right) and right-type squares (for rows where hole is to the left). However, the solution earlier argued that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column because that would force the hole to be within the tile. Let's examine that claim: Suppose a tile covers column j only (c = d = j). For a row i where p(i) > j, tile includes column j which is left of the hole (since j < p(i)). For a row i' where p(i') < j, tile includes column j which is right of the hole (since j > p(i')). So the tile includes squares left of the hole for some rows and right of the hole for others. Does that cause the tile to contain the hole? The hole is at (i,p(i)) for each row i. The tile includes only column j, so the hole (i,p(i)) is not in the tile unless p(i) = j. But for rows where p(i) > j, p(i) != j; for rows where p(i) < j, also not j. So the tile does not contain any hole. So the tile can contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column, as long as it does not include the hole column. So the claim that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column is false. Wait, the solution says: \"A tile cannot contain both a left‑type square and a right‑type square in the same column, because that would force the hole $(i,p(i))$ to belong to $[c,d]$.\" Let's examine that statement. If a tile covers column j only, then for rows i where p(i) > j, tile includes left-type squares; for rows i where p(i) < j, tile includes right-type squares. The hole column p(i) is not j for those rows, so the tile does not contain the hole. So the claim is false. However, the solution's later argument about column j requiring two distinct tiles may still hold because they require a tile covering left-type squares in column j must have rightmost column j, and a tile covering right-type squares in column j must have leftmost column j. Could a single tile with both leftmost and rightmost column j (i.e., width 1) serve both roles? Let's examine: If a tile has width 1 (c = d = j), then its leftmost column is j and its rightmost column is also j. So it could be considered both a tile with leftmost column j (covering right-type squares) and a tile with rightmost column j (covering left-type squares). So a single tile could potentially cover both left-type and right-type squares in column j, thus fulfilling both requirements. The solution's claim that the two tiles must be distinct may be invalid. However, the solution's lower bound argument says \"there must exist a tile whose rightmost column is j (to cover the left‑type squares of column j); there must exist a (different) tile whose leftmost column is j (to cover the right‑type squares of column j).\" They assert the tile must be different. But is that necessarily true? Could one tile have both leftmost and rightmost column j? That would be a tile of width 1. Does such a tile cover both left-type and right-type squares? Let's examine the definitions: left-type squares are those squares (i,j) where j < p(i) (hole to the right). R