e it's right of the hole, because then the tile would intersect the hole for some row? Actually, the tile would be left of the hole for some rows and right of the hole for others, but still not contain any hole. However, the tile would not satisfy either condition (1) or (2) for all rows, because condition (1) requires d < p(i) for all i in [a,b]; condition (2) requires c > p(i) for all i in [a,b]. If we have rows i where p(i) > j and rows i' where p(i') < j, then for those rows, condition (1) fails for i' (since d=j is not less than p(i') which is < j) and condition (2) fails for i (since c=j is not greater than p(i) which is > j). So the tile does not satisfy either condition for all rows, thus the tile would be invalid according to the earlier definition. But is it allowed for a tile to have rows where both conditions hold? The solution's definition of left-type and right-type squares is based on the tile's location relative to the hole for each row. They claim that for a tile to not contain any hole, for each row i in its interval, either the tile is entirely left of the hole (i.e., d < p(i)) or entirely right of the hole (c > p(i)). However, they didn't require the same condition for all rows; they said \"For any row i in [a,b] the uncovered square in that row is (i,p(i)). Since T may not contain an uncovered square, we must have for every i in [a,b] either d < p(i) or c > p(i).\" Actually, they wrote: \"Since $T$ may not contain an uncovered square, we must have for every $i\\in[a,b]$ either  \n\n\\[\nd<p(i)\\qquad\\text{(the whole tile lies left of the hole in row }i\\text{)}\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nor  \n\n\\[\nc>p(i)\\qquad\\text{(the whole tile lies right of the hole in row }i\\text{)}.\\tag{2}\n\\]\"\n\nThus they allow for each row i, either condition (1) or (2) could hold, potentially varying by row. But then they later define left-type and right-type tiles as those where the tile satisfies (1) for all its rows (left-type) or (2) for all its rows (right-type). They say: \"Thus a tile can cover only squares of one of the two types in a given column: left-type – the tile satisfies (1) for all its rows; right-type – the tile satisfies (2) for all its rows.\" They claim \"A tile cannot contain both a left‑type square and a right‑type square in the same column, because that would force the hole $(i,p(i))$ to belong to $[c,d]$.\" However, as we reasoned, a tile could have rows where condition (1) holds and rows where condition (2) holds, but then the tile would contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column. Does that force the hole to belong to [c,d]? Let's examine: Suppose tile T covers rows i in [a,b] and columns [c,d]. For a row i where condition (1) holds, we have d < p(i). For a row i' where condition (2) holds, we have c > p(i'). Since c ≤ d (by definition of interval), we have c ≤ d. If there exists i such that d < p(i) and i' such that c > p(i'), then we have c > p(i') and d < p(i). Since c ≤ d, we get p(i') < c ≤ d < p(i). So p(i') < p(i). So the holes for those rows are on opposite sides of the tile's column interval. The tile does not contain any hole because for each row i, the tile's columns are either entirely left of the hole (if d < p(i)) or entirely right of the hole (if c > p(i')). So the tile does not contain any hole. So it's possible for a tile to have rows where condition (1) holds and rows where condition (2) holds, as long as the tile's column interval lies between the holes for those rows. But the tile's column interval is fixed; for any row i where p(i) ≤ c, condition (2) holds; for any row i where p(i) ≥ d+1, condition (1) holds. So if there are rows with holes on both sides of the tile, the tile can be placed in the \"gap\" between the holes. However, the tile's rows must be contiguous (since it's a rectangle). So the rows in the tile must be a contiguous interval [a,b] such that all holes for rows in [a,b] are either all > d (to the right) or all < c (to the left), or some are on b