 could the same tile serve as both a tile with rightmost column j and leftmost column j? That would require the tile's leftmost column = rightmost column = j (width 1). So a width-1 tile could be both. But would it be able to cover at least one left-type square and at least one right-type square? Yes, if the tile's row interval includes at least one row from L_j and at least one row from R_j. But can a tile include rows from both L_j and R_j? As we argued, a tile can include rows where holes are on both sides, as long as the tile's column interval lies between the holes. For a width-1 tile at column j, the column interval is just j. For rows i in L_j (p(i) > j), condition (1) holds: d=j < p(i). For rows i in R_j (p(i) < j), condition (2) holds: c=j > p(i). So the tile can include rows from both L_j and R_j. However, does the tile's row interval need to be contiguous? Yes. So we need a contiguous interval of rows that includes at least one row from L_j and at least one row from R_j. Since L_j and R_j are separated by the row i0 where p(i0)=j, we could have a tile covering rows that include i0? No, because tile cannot include the uncovered square at (i0,j). So the tile's row interval cannot include i0. So the tile's rows must be either entirely above i0 or entirely below i0 (or possibly both if we skip i0, but row interval must be contiguous, so cannot skip i0). So a tile cannot include rows from both L_j and R_j because those rows are separated by i0 (the row where p(i)=j). Since the tile cannot include i0, the row interval cannot cross i0. Therefore, any tile can only include rows from either L_j or R_j, not both. So a width-1 tile cannot cover both left-type and right-type squares for column j. So the solution's claim that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column is indeed true under the additional constraint that the tile cannot contain the uncovered square (i0,j). Because to include rows from both L_j and R_j, the tile's row 