                            "text": "The problem: We have a 2025x2025 grid of unit squares. We need to place rectangular tiles (axis-aligned) covering unit squares, each tile's sides lie on grid lines, each unit square is covered at most once. We need to have each row and each column have exactly one unit square not covered by any tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles needed.\n\nThe solution: They define n=2025, p(i) is column of unique uncovered square in row i. Since each column also has exactly one uncovered square, p is a permutation. Then they analyze geometry of tiles: each tile is axis-aligned rectangle covering rows [a,b] and columns [c,d]. For each row i in [a,b], the uncovered square is (i,p(i)). Since tile cannot contain uncovered square, either all p(i) > d (hole to the right of tile) or all p(i) < c (hole to left of tile). So tile can only cover squares left of holes (left-type) or right of holes (right-type). Then they define L_j = { i | p(i) > j } (rows where hole is to the right of column j) and R_j = { i | p(i) < j } (rows where hole is to the left of column j). They compute sizes: |L_j| = n - j, |R_j| = j - 1. Then they argue that any tile covering a left-type square in column j must have rightmost column exactly j, and any tile covering a right-type square in column j must have leftmost column exactly j. So each column j (2 <= j <= n-1) has both left and right type squares, requiring at least two distinct tiles: one with rightmost column j, one with leftmost column j. For j=1 and j=n, only one tile needed each. So total >= 2(n-2) + 2 = 2(n-1). Then they give a construction achieving this bound: choose identity permutation p(i)=i, uncovered squares are diagonal. Then for each column j, define left region L_j = { (i,j) | i=j+1,...,n } (vertical rectangle width 1, height n-j). For each column j, define right region R_j = { (i,j) | i=1,...,j-1 } (vertical rectangle width 1, height j-1). These are vertical rectangles covering all squares except diagonal. They are disjoint. So total tiles = (n-1)+(n-1) = 2(n-1). So minimal number = 2(n-1) = 4048.\n\nThus answer: 4048.\n\nWe need to evaluate correctness and reasoning.\n\nFirst, check answer correctness: The answer is 4048. Is that correct? Let's think about the problem. We need to cover all squares except exactly one per row and column. So we need to leave exactly n uncovered squares (one per row and column). The uncovered squares must form a permutation matrix (like a rook placement). So we need to tile the rest of the board with axis-aligned rectangles, each covering squares, not overlapping. The goal is to minimize number of tiles.\n\nThe solution claims lower bound 2(n-1). Is that correct? Let's try to think about possible tilings. The uncovered squares are a permutation matrix. The rest of the board is the complement of that permutation matrix. The complement consists of n^2 - n squares. We need to cover them with rectangles, each rectangle cannot include any uncovered square. So each rectangle must lie entirely in the region left or right of each uncovered square in each row. The solution's argument that each column j (2 <= j <= n-1) must have at least one left-type tile (rightmost column j) and one right-type tile (leftmost column j) seems plausible. But need to verify that the tile covering left-type squares in column j must have rightmost column exactly j. Let's examine: Suppose tile T covers a left-type square in column j. That means for each row i in its row interval [a,b], we have d < p(i). Since p(i) > j (hole to right of column j) for those rows, we have d < p(i). But we also have that tile includes column j, so j <= d (since tile covers column j). Actually tile covers column j, so j is between c and d inclusive. Since tile cannot contain any uncovered square, we need d < p(i) for all i in [a,b]. Since p(i) > j for those rows (by definition of left-type squares), the maximum possible d such that d < p(i) for all i is the minimum of p(i) across those rows minus 1. But they argue that d must be exactly j. Let's examine: Suppose we have a tile covering column j and also some columns to the left of j (c <= j <= d). Since tile cannot contain any uncovered squares, for each row i in [a,b], we need either d < p(i) (hole to right) or c > p(i) (hole to left). For left-type squares, we have d < p(i). So for each row i in [a,b], we have p(i) > d. Since tile includes column j, we have j <= d. So p(i) > d >= j, so p(i) > j. So indeed left-type squares are those rows where p(i) > j. So the set of rows in tile must be a subset of L_j. So [a,b] subset of L_j. Now, can d be > j? If d > j, then tile covers column d > j. Since tile's rows are subset of L_j, for each row i in [a,b], p(i) > d? Actually we need d < p(i). If d > j, we need p(i) > d > j. So for each row i in [a,b], p(i) must be > d. But p(i) > j for all rows in L_j, but not necessarily > d. For some rows i in L_j, p(i) may be j+1, j+2, etc. So if we choose d > j, we need to restrict rows to those with p(i) > d. That is possible: we could have a tile covering a subset of rows where p(i) > d, and columns from c to d. That tile would be left-type and have rightmost column d > j, covering column j also? Wait, if d > j, tile includes column j only if c <= j <= d. So if d > j, tile includes column j if c <= j. But then for rows where p(i) > d, we have p(i) > d > j, so p(i) > j, fine. So tile could have rightmost column > j, covering column j. But the solution claims that any tile covering a left-type square in column j must have rightmost column exactly j. Let's test with a simple example: n=5, p = identity (holes at (i,i)). For column j=2, left-type rows are L_2 = {i | p(i) > 2} = {3,4,5}. So left-type squares in column 2 are (3,2), (4,2), (5,2). The solution's construction uses L_2 = vertical rectangle covering rows 3-5, column 2, with rightmost column = 2. That's one tile. Could we have a tile covering column 2 and also column 3, say covering rows 3-5, columns 2-3? Let's see: For rows 3-5, p(i) = i > 3? Actually p(3)=3, p(4)=4, p(5)=5. For tile covering columns 2-3, rightmost column d=3. For each row i=3, p(3)=3, which is not > d=3 (p(i) = d). The condition for left-type is d < p(i), i.e., p(i) > d. Since p(3)=3 = d, fails. So tile cannot include row 3. Could we restrict rows to 4-5? For rows 4-5, p(4)=4 > 3, p(5)=5 > 3, so d=3 < p(i) holds. So tile covering rows 4-5, columns 2-3 would be left-type for those rows. Does it cover column 2? Yes. So tile covering rows 4-5, columns 2-3 is a left-type tile covering column 2, with rightmost column d=3 > j=2. So this tile would be a left-type tile covering column 2 but with rightmost column 3. Does this violate any constraints? It would also cover column 3 squares (4,3) and (5,3). Are those squares left-type squares for column 3? For column 3, left-type rows are L_3 = {i | p(i) > 3} = {4,5}. So indeed squares (4,3) and (5,3) are left-type squares for column 3. So this tile would be covering left-type squares for both column 2 and column 3. That's allowed. However, the solution's argument says that any tile covering a left-type square in column j must have rightmost column exactly j. But we have a counterexample: tile covering rows 4-5, columns 2-3, covering left-type squares in column 2, but rightmost column is 3 > 2. So the claim seems false. But perhaps the solution's argument implicitly assumes that the tile covers *all* left-type squares in column j? Let's read carefully: \"If T covers a left‑type square in column j then (1) holds for every row i of T, i.e. d<p(i) for all i∈[a,b]. Since all those rows belong to L_j, we have [a,b]⊆L_j. Because d is the rightmost column of T, d must be the largest column that can be used while staying left of all holes in [a,b]; hence d=j.\" This reasoning is flawed: d must be ≤ min_{i∈[a,b]} p(i) - 1. The minimum p(i) among rows in [a,b] could be > j+1, so d could be > j. The claim that the largest column that can be used while staying left of all holes in [a,b] is j is not necessarily true; the largest column that can be used is min(p(i))-1, which could be > j. So the lower bound argument that each column j forces a tile with rightmost column exactly j is not correct. However, perhaps they consider that we need at least one tile with rightmost column j for each column j, but not necessarily that each tile covering left-type squares in column j must have rightmost column exactly j. Actually they argue that for each column j, there must exist a tile whose rightmost column is j (to cover left-type squares of column j). But we could have a tile covering left-type squares of column j and also covering left-type squares of column j+1, etc., with rightmost column > j. That tile would still cover left-type squares of column j, but its rightmost column is not j. However, maybe there is still a need for a tile with rightmost column j because left-type squares of column j that are not covered by any tile with rightmost column > j might need a tile with rightmost column j. But could we cover all left-type squares of column j using tiles whose rightmost column > j? Let's think: Suppose we have a tile covering rows [a,b] and columns [c,d] with d > j, covering column j. For each row i in [a,b], we need d < p(i). So p(i) > d > j. So the rows in [a,b] are a subset of rows where p(i) > d. So they are rows where the hole is to the right of column d. So these rows also have holes to the right of column j, but also to the right of column d. So they are a subset of L_j, but also a subset of L_d. So the tile covers left-type squares of column j (and also left-type squares of columns up to d). So it's possible that a tile with rightmost column d > j covers left-type squares of column j. So the existence of a tile with rightmost column j is not forced by column j alone; we could have a tile with rightmost column > j covering those squares. However, perhaps the tile's leftmost column must be ≤ j, but that doesn't guarantee a tile with rightmost column exactly j. So the lower bound argument might be flawed.\n\nBut we need to examine the entire argument: They claim that each column j (2 ≤ j ≤ n-1) forces two distinct tiles: one with rightmost column j (cover left-type squares) and one with leftmost column j (cover right-type squares). This is a claim that may not hold. Let's test with small n to see if we can find a tiling with fewer than 2(n-1) tiles. For n=3, 2(n-1) = 4. Is it possible to tile a 3x3 grid leaving one uncovered per row and column with fewer than 4 tiles? Let's try to find a tiling with 3 tiles. The uncovered squares must be a permutation of size 3. Let's try identity permutation: uncovered squares at (1,1), (2,2), (3,3). The complement consists of all squares except diagonal. That's 6 squares. Could we tile them with 3 rectangles? Possibly: For column 1, left region squares are (2,1), (3,1). For column 2, left region squares are (3,2). Right region squares: column 2: (1,2). Column 3: right region squares are (1,3), (2,3). The solution's construction uses vertical rectangles: L1: (2,1),(3,1) (size 2x1), L2: (3,2) (size 1x1), L3: none? Actually L3 is empty because j=n? Wait for n=3, L1 = rows 2-3 column 1 (vertical rectangle), L2 = row 3 column 2 (vertical rectangle), L3 = empty (since j=n). Right region: R2 = row 1 column 2 (vertical rectangle), R3 = rows 1-2 column 3 (vertical rectangle). So total tiles = (n-1)+(n-1)=4. So they achieve 4 tiles. Could we do with 3? Let's try to combine some vertical rectangles: maybe we can have a rectangle covering (2,1),(3,1),(3,2) (a shape L-shaped? But rectangles must be axis-aligned, so can't be L-shaped. Could we have a rectangle covering rows 2-3, columns 1-2? That would be a 2x2 rectangle covering squares (2,1),(2,2),(3,1),(3,2). But (2,2) is an uncovered square (hole). So not allowed. Could we have a rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 2-3? That would include (2,2) uncovered. Not allowed. Could we have a rectangle covering rows 1-3, column 2? That would be a 3x1 rectangle covering (1,2),(2,2),(3,2). But (2,2) is uncovered. Not allowed. So any rectangle covering column 2 must avoid row 2. So you could have rectangle covering rows 1 and 3, column 2? But rectangle must be contiguous rows, so cannot skip row 2. So column 2 cannot be covered by a rectangle of height >1 unless it avoids row 2, which is impossible. So column 2 must be covered by two separate rectangles: one covering (1,2) and one covering (3,2). Actually (3,2) is left-type square, (1,2) is right-type square. So we need at least two tiles for column 2. Similarly column 1: left-type squares (2,1),(3,1) can be covered by one tile (vertical rectangle). Right-type squares for column 1: none. So column 1 needs at least one tile. Column 3: left-type squares none, right-type squares (1,3),(2,3) need at least one tile (vertical rectangle). So total at least 1+2+1 = 4 tiles. So lower bound 4 holds for n=3. So the solution's lower bound seems correct for n=3.\n\nBut the argument that each column j forces a tile with rightmost column exactly j might be too strong, but the conclusion that there must be at least one tile covering left-type squares of column j and at least one covering right-type squares of column j may still hold. The existence of a tile covering left-type squares of column j does not require that its rightmost column is j; it could be larger. However, perhaps we can argue that if there is a tile covering left-type squares of column j with rightmost column > j, then that tile also covers left-type squares of column j+1, j+2, ... up to its rightmost column. So each tile covering left-type squares for column j also covers left-type squares for all columns up to its rightmost column. So we could potentially reduce the number of tiles needed for left-type squares across multiple columns by using a tile that spans multiple columns. However, the tile must also avoid uncovered squares in those rows. The uncovered squares are at positions (i,p(i)). If p(i) = i (identity), then left-type rows for column j are those with i > j. So a tile covering rows i = j+1,...,n and columns j,...,d (with d > j) would need to avoid uncovered squares at (i,i) for i in rows j+1,...,n. Since the tile includes column i for each row i? Wait, tile includes columns j,...,d. For row i, the uncovered square is at column i. If i is within [j,d], then the tile would include the uncovered square (i,i) which is not allowed. So to avoid uncovered squares, the tile's column interval must be entirely to the left of each row's uncovered column. For row i, uncovered column is i. So tile's rightmost column d must be < i for each row i in the tile. So d < min_{i∈[a,b]} i. So d must be less than the smallest row index in the tile. Since rows are contiguous, the smallest row index is a. So d < a. So tile covering rows a,...,b must have rightmost column < a. That means tile's columns are to the left of the smallest row index. So for identity permutation, any left-type tile covering rows a,...,b must have columns ≤ a-1. So the rightmost column of the tile is at most a-1. But also tile must cover column j, so j ≤ d ≤ a-1. So a ≥ j+1. So the tile's rows must start at a ≥ j+1. So the tile can cover rows a,...,b where a ≥ j+1. So the tile's rightmost column is ≤ a-1, which could be > j if a > j+1. For example, if we take a = j+2, then d ≤ a-1 = j+1, so d could be j+1 > j. So tile could have rightmost column j+1. But then tile's rows start at j+2, so it does not cover row j+1. So left-type squares for column j are rows i > j. So tile covering rows j+2,...,b would cover left-type squares for column j (since those rows have holes at columns > j). So tile could have rightmost column j+1, covering column j for rows j+2,...,b. But would it also cover column j+1 for those rows? Yes. But we need to ensure that column j+1's left-type squares for those rows are also covered. For row i = j+2, uncovered column is i = j+2 > j+1, so column j+1 is left of uncovered column, so tile covering column j+1 is okay. So tile could have rightmost column j+1. So indeed a tile covering left-type squares for column j could have rightmost column > j. So the claim that each column j forces a tile with rightmost column exactly j is not necessary. However, the lower bound might still hold because we need to cover all left-type squares across all columns, and each tile can only cover left-type squares for a contiguous range of columns, but maybe we can cover many columns with a single tile, reducing the number of needed tiles. The solution's lower bound may be too high.\n\nWe need to examine the problem more thoroughly: The goal is to minimize number of tiles. The solution claims minimal number is 2(n-1). Is that indeed minimal? Let's try to see if we can do better for n=4 or n=5. Let's attempt to find a tiling with fewer than 2(n-1) = 6 for n=4. For n=4, identity permutation yields uncovered squares at (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(4,4). The complement consists of 12 squares. The solution's construction uses vertical rectangles: L1: rows 2-4 col1 (3 squares), L2: rows 3-4 col2 (2 squares), L3: row4 col3 (1 square) -> total left region tiles = 3, right region tiles: R2: row1 col2 (1), R3: rows1-2 col3 (2), R4: rows1-3 col4 (3) -> total right region tiles = 3. So total 6 tiles.\n\nCould we tile with fewer? Let's try to combine some vertical rectangles into larger rectangles that span multiple columns. For identity permutation, left-type squares are below diagonal, right-type squares are above diagonal. The region below diagonal is a triangular region of squares (i > j). The region above diagonal is triangular region (i < j). These are two triangular halves. We need to tile each half with axis-aligned rectangles that do not include diagonal squares. The minimal number of rectangles needed to tile a triangular region of an n x n grid (excluding diagonal) is known to be n-1 (for each half). Actually, the solution uses n-1 vertical rectangles for each half. Could we tile the lower triangular region with fewer than n-1 rectangles? Let's think: The lower triangular region (including squares (i,j) with i > j) is a shape that is like a staircase. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a staircase shape is indeed n-1 (if you use rectangles that are \"staircase\" shape). But maybe you could use larger rectangles that cover multiple columns and rows. However, any rectangle in the lower triangular region must avoid diagonal squares. For a rectangle covering rows a..b and columns c..d, we need for each row i in [a,b], the uncovered column p(i) = i must be > d (hole to the right) because the rectangle is left of the diagonal. So we need i > d for all i in [a,b]. So the condition is that the smallest row index a must be > d. So d < a. So the rectangle's columns must be strictly less than the smallest row index of the rectangle. So the rectangle can be placed anywhere left of the diagonal, but the columns must be less than the smallest row index. So the rectangle's width can be up to a-1 columns (from 1 to a-1). So for each rectangle, the rightmost column is at most a-1, where a is the top row of the rectangle. So we can have a rectangle that covers rows a..b and columns 1..(a-1). That's a rectangle that covers the entire left region for rows a..b. For a=2, that rectangle would be rows 2..b, columns 1..1 (vertical rectangle). For a=3, columns 1..2, rows 3..b. So we could have a rectangle covering rows 3..4, columns 1..2 (2x2 rectangle). But does that include any diagonal squares? For rows 3 and 4, diagonal squares are at (3,3) and (4,4). The rectangle columns are 1..2, so it does not include those. So it's allowed. So we could have a rectangle covering rows 3..4, columns 1..2, covering squares (3,1),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2). That's 4 squares. In the solution's construction, those squares are covered by L1 (rows 2-4 col1) and L2 (rows 3-4 col2). L1 covers (2,1),(3,1),(4,1). L2 covers (3,2),(4,2). So the solution uses 2 rectangles for those 4 squares. My rectangle would combine them into 1 rectangle, potentially reducing tile count. However, does this rectangle also cover any square that is right-type? No, it's left of diagonal, so it's fine. So we can combine L1 and L2 into a larger rectangle covering rows 3-4, columns 1-2. But note that L1 also includes row 2 col1, which is not covered by that rectangle. So we still need to cover (2,1). That could be covered by a rectangle rows 2..2, columns 1..1 (a single square). So we would have L1' covering row 2 col1 (size 1), and L2' covering rows 3-4 columns 1-2 (size 2x2). So total left region tiles = 2 (instead of 3). Similarly, we could combine right region tiles. For right region (above diagonal), we can combine vertical rectangles into larger rectangles that span multiple columns. For n=4, right region squares are (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,3),(2,4),(3,4). We could cover rows 1..1, columns 2..4 as a single rectangle (size 1x3) covering (1,2),(1,3),(1,4). That's allowed because row 1's uncovered column is 1, which is left of columns 2..4, so tile is right of diagonal (hole to left). So we can have a rectangle covering row 1, columns 2..4. Then we need to cover (2,3),(2,4) and (3,4). We could have rectangle rows 2..3, columns 4..4 (vertical rectangle covering (2,4),(3,4)). And rectangle rows 2..2, columns 3..3 (single square (2,3)). So total right region tiles = 3 (instead of 3). Actually we used 3 tiles: one covering row1 col2-4, one covering rows2-3 col4, one covering row2 col3. That's still 3, same as original. But maybe we can combine further: Could we have rectangle rows 2..3, columns 3..4? That would include (2,3),(2,4),(3,3),(3,4). But (3,3) is a diagonal square (uncovered) not allowed. So cannot include column 3 for row 3 because diagonal at (3,3). So we cannot combine (2,3) and (3,4) into a single rectangle without covering a diagonal. So maybe minimal number of rectangles for right region is also n-1. But left region we reduced from 3 to 2 for n=4. Let's see if we can reduce further: Could we have rectangle rows 2..4, columns 1..2? That would include row 2 col2, which is a diagonal square (2,2) not allowed. So cannot include column 2 for row 2. So rectangle rows 2..4, columns 1..2 is invalid because row 2's uncovered column is 2, which is within columns 1..2, so tile would contain uncovered square. So we cannot include column 2 for row 2. So the rectangle rows 3..4, columns 1..2 is maximal for those rows. So left region minimal number of rectangles might be n-1 as well? Let's try to find a systematic minimal tiling for triangular region. This is reminiscent of the problem of partitioning a Young diagram shape (staircase) into rectangles. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a staircase shape of size n (i.e., shape with rows of lengths 1,2,...,n-1) is known to be n-1. Actually, the shape of lower triangular region (excluding diagonal) is a Young diagram with row i having i-1 squares (for i from 2 to n). The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a Young diagram is equal to the number of rows (or columns) in the diagram? Let's recall known results: The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a Young diagram (Ferrers diagram) is equal to the size of the largest antichain in the poset of cells (by inclusion). For a staircase shape, the largest antichain size is floor(n/2) maybe? Actually, the shape is a Ferrers diagram with row lengths decreasing: row 1 has n-1 squares, row 2 has n-2, ..., row n-1 has 1. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition this shape is known to be n-1 (I think). But we need to be careful: The shape we have is lower triangular region: rows i (i from 2 to n) have i-1 squares left of diagonal. That's a shape with row lengths: row 2:1, row3:2,..., row n: n-1. That's a staircase shape rotated 90 degrees: it's the shape of a Young diagram with column lengths: column 1 has n-1 squares, column 2 has n-2, ..., column n-1 has 1. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a staircase shape is indeed n-1: you can partition it into n-1 rectangles each of size 1x(k) or kx1 (vertical strips). But can you partition into fewer rectangles? Possibly not. Let's think: The shape is a Ferrers diagram that is a \"staircase\" shape. There is a known result: The rectangle covering number (or partition number) of a Ferrers diagram equals the number of rows (or columns) of the diagram. Actually, the rectangle covering number (minimum number of rectangles needed to cover all cells without overlap) for a Ferrers diagram is equal to the number of rows (or columns) of the diagram. For a staircase shape of size n-1 rows, the number is n-1. Indeed, you cannot cover the shape with fewer rectangles because each rectangle can cover at most one cell in the main diagonal of the shape? Not exactly. Let's examine a small case: n=4, shape has rows lengths 1,2,3. Can we partition it into 2 rectangles? The shape has 6 cells. Could we partition into 2 rectangles? For example, rectangle covering rows 3-4, columns 1-2 (size 2x2) covers 4 cells: (3,1),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2). The remaining cells are (2,1) and (4,3). (4,3) is not in shape? Actually shape includes (4,1),(4,2),(4,3) because row 4 has 3 squares left of diagonal: columns 1,2,3. So after covering rectangle rows 3-4, columns 1-2, we have left (2,1) and (4,3). These cannot be covered by a single rectangle because they are not aligned: they are not in same row or column. So need at least 3 rectangles. So minimal is 3 = n-1. So for lower triangular shape, minimal rectangles = n-1. Similarly for upper triangular shape (right region). So total minimal rectangles for both halves = 2(n-1). So the lower bound seems plausible.\n\nBut we need to verify that the lower bound argument is correct in general, not just for identity permutation. The solution's argument uses a generic permutation p, but the lower bound may depend on p. However, they claim that any permutation yields at least 2(n-1) tiles. Let's test with a different permutation, maybe we can find a tiling with fewer tiles. For example, consider p(i) = n+1-i (reverse diagonal). Then uncovered squares are anti-diagonal. The complement shape is also two triangular halves but rotated. The shape is symmetric. The same argument likely yields 2(n-1). But perhaps there is a permutation that yields a shape that can be tiled with fewer rectangles. Let's think about a permutation that is not monotonic, like p(i) = i for i odd, p(i) = i+1 for i even (or something). The complement shape might be more \"scattered\". Could that allow fewer rectangles? Let's explore.\n\nWe need to tile the complement of a permutation matrix (n x n grid with n holes). The complement consists of n^2 - n squares. The holes are at positions (i,p(i)). The complement is the union of left region squares (i,j) where j < p(i) and right region squares (i,j) where j > p(i). So each row i is divided into left region (columns 1..p(i)-1) and right region (columns p(i)+1..n). These are contiguous intervals left and right of the hole. So each row has two intervals: left interval of length p(i)-1 and right interval of length n-p(i). The complement is the union of these intervals across rows.\n\nWe need to partition this region into axis-aligned rectangles that do not intersect any hole. The rectangles can be placed anywhere, but must avoid holes.\n\nThe lower bound argument: For each column j (2 ≤ j ≤ n-1), there are left-type squares (rows where p(i) > j) and right-type squares (rows where p(i) < j). So each column j has at least one left-type square and at least one right-type square. The solution claims that each left-type square in column j must be covered by a tile whose rightmost column is j, and each right-type square must be covered by a tile whose leftmost column is j. This is not necessarily true as we saw. However, perhaps the argument can be salvaged: Even if a tile covering left-type squares of column j has rightmost column > j, then that tile also covers left-type squares of column j+1,...,d. So maybe we can \"charge\" that tile to the rightmost column it covers. Then each column j must be the rightmost column of some tile covering left-type squares of column j (or a later column). But we need to ensure that each column j is the rightmost column of at least one tile covering left-type squares of some column ≤ j. Actually, we need to count tiles based on rightmost columns. For each tile covering left-type squares, define its rightmost column d. Then that tile covers left-type squares for all columns j ≤ d (provided those columns have left-type squares in the rows of the tile). So each tile can be assigned to its rightmost column d. Then we need at least one tile for each column j from 1 to n-1? Let's think: For column 1, left-type squares exist for rows where p(i) > 1. So we need at least one tile covering left-type squares of column 1. That tile's rightmost column could be any column d ≥ 1. But we can assign that tile to its rightmost column d. So we need at least one tile for each possible rightmost column value? Not exactly. The number of distinct rightmost columns among tiles covering left-type squares is at most the number of tiles. So if we can have a tile with rightmost column = n-1 covering left-type squares for all columns 1..n-1, then we would have only one tile covering left-type squares for all columns. But is that possible? Let's examine constraints: A tile covering left-type squares must have rows [a,b] such that for each row i in [a,b], p(i) > d (rightmost column). So the rows must be those where hole column > d. So the tile's rows are a subset of rows where p(i) > d. So if we want d = n-1, we need rows where p(i) > n-1, i.e., p(i) = n. So only rows where the hole is at column n can be included. Typically there may be only one such row (since p is a permutation). So a tile with rightmost column n-1 can only cover rows where p(i) = n. That's at most one row. So that tile would be at most height 1. So it cannot cover many rows. So we cannot have a single tile covering left-type squares for all columns. In general, the set of rows where"
