ept diagonal. They are disjoint. So total tiles = (n-1)+(n-1) = 2(n-1). So minimal number = 2(n-1) = 4048.\n\nThus answer: 4048.\n\nWe need to evaluate correctness and reasoning.\n\nFirst, check answer correctness: The answer is 4048. Is that correct? Let's think about the problem. We need to cover all squares except exactly one per row and column. So we need to leave exactly n uncovered squares (one per row and column). The uncovered squares must form a permutation matrix (like a rook placement). So we need to tile the rest of the board with axis-aligned rectangles, each covering squares, not overlapping. The goal is to minimize number of tiles.\n\nThe solution claims lower bound 2(n-1). Is that correct? Let's try to think about possible tilings. The uncovered squares are a permutation matrix. The rest of the board is the complement of that permutation matrix. The complement consists of n^2 - n squares. We need to cover them with rectangles, each rectangle cannot include any uncovered square. So each rectangle must lie entirely in the region left or right of each uncovered square in each row. The solution's argument that each column j (2 <= j <= n-1) must have at least one left-type tile (rightmost column j) and one right-type tile (leftmost column j) seems plausible. But need to verify that the tile covering left-type squares in column j must have rightmost column exactly j. Let's examine: Suppose tile T covers a left-type square in column j. That means for each row i in its row interval [a,b], we have d < p(i). Since p(i) > j (hole to right of column j) for those rows, we have d < p(i). But we also have that tile includes column j, so j <= d (since tile covers column j). Actually tile covers column j, so j is between c and d inclusive. Since tile cannot contain any uncovered square, we need d < p(i) for all i in [a,b]. Since p(i) > j for those rows (by definition of left-type squares), the maximum possible d such that d < p(i) for all i is the minimum of p(i) across those rows minus 1. But they argue that d must be exactly j. Let's examine: Suppose we have a tile covering column j and also some columns to the left of j (c <= j <= d). Since tile cannot contain any uncovered squares, for each row i in [a,b], we need either d < p(i) (hole to right) or c > p(i) (hole to left). For left-type squares, we have d < p(i). So for each row i in [a,b], we have p(i) > d. Since tile includes column j, we have j <= d. So p(i) > d >= j, so p(i) > j. So indeed left-type squares are those rows where p(i) > j. So the set of rows in tile must be a subset of L_j. So [a,b] subset of L_j. Now, can d be > j? If d > j, then tile covers column d > j. Since tile's rows are subset of L_j, for each row i in [a,b], p(i) > d? Actually we need d < p(i). If d > j, we need p(i) > d > j. So for each row i in [a,b], p(i) must be > d. But p(i) > j for all rows in L_j, but not necessarily > d. For some rows i in L_j, p(i) may be j+1, j+2, etc. So if we choose d > j, we need to restrict rows to those with p(i) > d. That is possible: we could have a tile covering a subset of rows where p(i) > d, and columns from c to d. That tile would be left-type and have rightmost column d > j, covering column j also? Wait, if d > j, tile includes column j only if c <= j <= d. So if d > j, tile includes column j if c <= j. But then for rows where p(i) > d, we have p(i) > d > j, so p(i) > j, fine. So tile could have rightmost column > j, covering column j. But the solution claims that any tile covering a left-type square in column j must have rightmost column exactly j. Let's test with a simple example: n=5, p = identity (holes at (i,i)). For column j=2, left-type rows are L_2 = {i | p(i) > 2} = {3,4,5}. So left-type squares in column 2 are (3,2), (4,2), (5,2). The solution's construction uses L_2 = vertical rectangle covering rows 3-5, column 2, with rightmost column = 2. That's one tile. Could we have a tile covering column 2 and also column 3, say covering rows 3-5, columns 