d argument: For each column j (2 ≤ j ≤ n-1), there are left-type squares (rows where p(i) > j) and right-type squares (rows where p(i) < j). So each column contains both left and right squares, requiring at least one tile that ends at column j (rightmost column j) covering left squares, and at least one tile that starts at column j covering right squares. So each column j (2..n-1) forces two distinct tiles. For j=1 and j=n, each forces one tile. So total at least 2(n-2)+2 = 2(n-1). This seems plausible.\n\nNow, is there any possibility of a tile covering left-type squares of multiple columns? The argument says for each column j, any tile covering left-type squares in column j must have rightmost column exactly j. So a tile covering left-type squares in column j cannot also cover left-type squares in column j+1 because its rightmost column is j. So each column's left-type squares require a distinct tile with rightmost column j. Similarly for right-type squares. So indeed each column j (2..n-1) forces two distinct tiles. So lower bound is correct.\n\nNow the construction: They choose identity permutation p(i)=i. Then left region squares are those below the diagonal (i > j) for column j, and right region squares are those above diagonal (i < j). They define L_j as vertical rectangle of width 1 (column j) covering rows i=j+1..n (i.e., below diagonal). That's a vertical rectangle of height n-j. Its rightmost column is j (since width 1). It covers left-type squares of column j. Similarly R_j is vertical rectangle of width 1 covering rows i=1..j-1 (above diagonal). Its leftmost column is j. So each column j has two vertical tiles (except j=1 and j=n where only one side exists). The tiles are disjoint because L_j is below diagonal, R_j is above diagonal. So total tiles = (n-1)+(n-1) = 2(n-1). So construction matches lower bound.\n\nThus answer appears correct.\n\nNow, we need to check reasoning validity: The solution uses case analysis, combinatorial counting, and a constructive example. The methods are valid. The solution includes a construction with explicit specification: vertical rectangles with explicit coordinates. So Level 2: methods are valid, and construction claims include concrete details (vertical rectangles defined by column and row intervals). So Level 2 passes.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation quality. Are there any justification gaps or critical errors? Let's examine the solution for any missing justification or ambiguous steps.\n\nThe solution defines p(i) as column of uncovered square in row i; correct. Then they argue that p is a permutation because each column also has exactly one uncovered square. That's correct.\n\nThey define left-type and right-type squares based on tile being left or right of holes. They claim a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in same column because that would force the hole to be inside the tile. That's correct.\n\nThen they define L_j and R_j sets of rows with p(i)>j and p(i)<j. They compute sizes: |L_j| = n - j, |R_j| = j - 1. That's correct.\n\nThen they argue that any tile covering a left-type square in column j must have rightmost column exactly j. Let's verify: Suppose tile covers a left-type square at column j. Then tile's columns are [c,d] with d >= j (since it covers column j). Since tile is left-type, for all rows i in [a,b] (tile's rows), we have d < p(i). Since p(i) > j for those rows (by left-type definition), we have d < p(i). But we need to argue that d = j. Actually, they argue that d must be the largest column that can be used while staying left of all holes in [a,b]; thus d=j. But is that always true? Let's examine: Suppose tile covers left-type squares in column j, meaning for each row i in tile, p(i) > j. The tile's rightmost column d must be less than p(i) for all i in tile. So d < min_{i∈[a,b]} p(i). Since p(i) > j for all i in tile, min p(i) >= j+1. So d ≤ min p(i) - 1. Could d be less than j? If d < j, then tile would not cover column j (contradiction). So d must be exactly j. So yes, d=j. That's correct.\n\nSimilarly for right-type squares, leftmost column c=j. So the argument is valid.\n\nThen they claim each column j (2..n-1) contains both left-type and right-type squares, thus requires two distinct tiles: one with rightmost column j, one with leftmost column j. Are the tiles distinct? Could a tile have both rightmost column j and leftmost column j? That would be a tile of width 1 (i.e., a vertical column). Could that tile cover both left-type and right-type squares? No, because left-type squares are those with p(i) > j, right-type squares are those with p(i) < j. A tile covering column j only (width 1) would have both leftmost and rightmost column j. But can it cover both left-type and right-type squares? It would need to contain squares (i,j) for rows i in both L_j and R_j. But L_j and R_j are disjoint sets of rows. So tile could cover both sets if it includes rows from both L_j and R_j. However, the tile would then contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column j. The solution earlier argued that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column, because that would force the hole to be inside the tile. Let's verify: If tile includes a row i from L_j (p(i) > j) and a row i' from R_j (p(i') < j), then tile includes squares (i,j) and (i',j). Since tile's column interval includes j, and its row interval includes both i and i', the tile includes both squares. For left-type row i, we have p(i) > j, so the hole in row i is at column p(i) > j, which is to the right of tile's column interval? Actually tile's column interval includes j, but could also include columns beyond j? Wait, tile's column interval is [c,d] and includes j. For left-type squares, we have d < p(i). Since p(i) > j, we can have d = j (as argued). So tile's rightmost column is j. For right-type squares, we have c > p(i'). Since p(i') < j, we can have c = j. So tile's leftmost column is j. So a tile that includes both left-type and right-type squares would need to have both leftmost column j and rightmost column j, i.e., width 1. That's possible. But does that tile contain any hole? For row i (left-type), hole at column p(i) > j, which is to the right of tile (since tile's rightmost column is j). So tile does not contain hole for that row. For row i' (right-type), hole at column p(i') < j, which is to the left of tile (since tile's leftmost column is j). So tile does not contain hole for that row either. So tile could contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column j, as long as the tile's column interval is exactly [j,j] (width 1). But the solution claims that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column because that would force the hole to belong to [c,d]. Let's examine that claim: If tile includes both left-type and right-type squares in column j, then for the left-type row i, we have p(i) > j, so hole is to the right of column j. For right-type row i', p(i') < j, hole is to the left. So hole is not in column j. So tile does not contain any hole. So the claim that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column is false? Let's think: The earlier claim about left-type and right-type squares was about a tile's column interval [c,d] relative to holes. For a tile covering a left-type square in column j, we have d < p(i) for all rows i in tile. For a tile covering a right-type square in column j, we have c > p(i) for all rows i in tile. If a tile includes both left-type and right-type rows, then we need both d < p(i) for left-type rows and c > p(i') for right-type rows. Since c ≤ d (tile's leftmost column ≤ rightmost column), we need c ≤ d. But we also have c > p(i') for right-type rows (p(i') < j) and d < p(i) for left-type rows (p(i) > j). So we could have c = d = j, which satisfies c > p(i') (since p(i') ≤ j-1) and d < p(i) (since p(i) ≥ j+1). So indeed a tile of width 1 can contain both left-type and right-type squares. So the claim that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column is not strictly correct. However, the solution uses this claim to argue that each column j forces two distinct tiles. But the existence of a width-1 tile that covers both left-type and right-type squares would contradict that each column j forces two distinct tiles. However, does the solution require the tiles to be distinct? It says \"there must exist a tile whose rightmost column is j (to cover left-type squares of column j); there must exist a (different) tile whose leftmost column is j (to cover right-type squares of column j).\" The argument for distinctness is based on the claim that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column. But as we just argued, a tile of width 1 could contain both types. But does that tile satisfy both conditions? Let's examine: If we have a tile of width 1 at column j, its leftmost column = rightmost column = j. It would be both a tile whose rightmost column is j and a tile whose leftmost column is j. So it could serve both purposes. So the claim that we need two distinct tiles per column might be false. However, the solution's construction uses two separate vertical tiles per column (one above diagonal, one below diagonal). But perhaps there could be a different arrangement that uses fewer tiles, e.g., using width-1 tiles that cover both left and right squares in the same column, thereby reducing the total number of tiles. Let's examine if that is possible while maintaining non-overlapping condition.\n\nWe need to tile all squares except the holes. The holes are at positions (i,p(i)). The left-type squares are those to the left of the hole in each row, right-type squares are those to the right. For each column j, left-type squares are rows i where p(i) > j (i.e., holes to the right), right-type squares are rows i where p(i) < j (holes to the left). So column j is partitioned into three parts: rows with holes above (i.e., i < something?), but more precisely, the rows are partitioned into L_j (holes to right) and R_j (holes to left). In the identity permutation case, L_j are rows i > j (below diagonal), R_j are rows i < j (above diagonal). So column j consists of squares above diagonal (right-type) and below diagonal (left-type). The diagonal square (i=j) is a hole. So column j has both left-type and right-type squares.\n\nNow, can we tile column j with a single width-1 tile covering both above and below diagonal squares (i.e., all rows except the hole)? That would be a tile covering the entire column j except the hole at row i=j. But a tile must be a rectangle, i.e., contiguous rows. The hole at row i=j splits the column into two intervals: rows 1..j-1 (above) and rows j+1..n (below). A rectangle cannot be non-contiguous; it must be a contiguous block of rows. So a single width-1 tile cannot cover both intervals because the hole is in the middle. So we cannot have a single tile covering both left-type and right-type squares in the same column because the hole splits the column into two separate intervals. However, could we have a tile that covers some of the left-type squares and some of the right-type squares but not the hole? For a tile to be a rectangle, its rows must be a contiguous interval [a,b]. If we try to include both a row from above the hole (i<j) and a row from below the hole (i>j), then the interval [a,b] would include the hole row j. Since the tile's column interval includes j, the tile would include the hole square (i=j, column j). That is not allowed. So any tile covering both a left-type row and a right-type row in the same column must include the hole row, which is forbidden. So the claim that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column is correct, because the hole lies between them, and any contiguous interval of rows covering both must include the hole. The earlier argument about width-1 tile fails because the tile's rows must be contiguous; to include rows from both sides of the hole, the interval must include the hole row. So indeed a width-1 tile cannot cover both sides because it would have to include the hole row. So the claim stands.\n\nThus the lower bound argument is valid.\n\nNow, the solution's construction uses vertical tiles of width 1 that cover either above or below diagonal, which are contiguous intervals, so they are valid rectangles.\n\nThus the solution is correct.\n\nNow we need to check for any justification gaps. The solution is fairly thorough. However, there might be some minor gaps: For example, in the lower bound argument, they claim that for each column j (2..n-1), there must exist a tile whose rightmost column is j and a different tile whose leftmost column is j. The distinctness is justified by the claim that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column. The proof of that claim is given earlier: \"A tile cannot contain both a left‑type square and a right‑type square in the same column, because that would force the hole (i,p(i)) to belong to [c,d].\" This is a correct justification: If a tile contains both left- and right-type squares in column j, then it contains squares (i1,j) and (i2,j) where p(i1) > j and p(i2) < j. Since rows i1 and i2 are in the tile's row interval, the interval includes all rows between i1 and i2, which includes the row where p(i) = j (the hole). Actually, we need to argue that there exists a row i0 such that p(i0) = j (the hole in column j). Since p is a permutation, there is exactly one row i0 with p(i0) = j. That row i0 is between i1 and i2 (if i1 < i0 < i2 or vice versa). Since the tile's row interval includes i1 and i2, it includes i0. And the tile's column interval includes column j, so tile would contain the hole (i0,j), which is forbidden. So indeed a tile cannot contain both left- and right-type squares in the same column. So the distinctness argument is valid.\n\nThus the lower bound argument is sound.\n\nNow the construction: They define L_j and R_j as vertical rectangles of width 1. They claim these are pairwise disjoint and cover all squares except diagonal. Indeed, L_j covers rows i=j+1..n in column j, and R_j covers rows i=1..j-1 in column j. So for each column j, L_j and R_j partition the column except the diagonal square (i=j). So they cover all squares except diagonal squares. So the construction is correct.\n\nThus the solution appears correct and reasoning valid.\n\nNow we need to check for any missing justification: The solution uses the fact that \"Since p is a permutation, exactly j values of p(i) are ≤ j and exactly n−j values are > j\". That's correct. The solution uses the fact that \"L_j is nonempty for j ≤ n-1\" and \"R_j is nonempty for j ≥ 2\". That's correct.\n\nPotential minor gaps: The solution states \"Since d is the rightmost column of T, d must be the largest column that can be used while staying left of all holes in [a,b]; hence d=j.\" This is a bit informal but the reasoning is clear: If d < j, then tile does not cover column j; if d > j, then tile would include a hole. So d=j. Similarly for c=j. This is a justification gap? It's a minor step but logically sound. According to the guidelines, missing details that are straightforward are considered Justification Gaps, not Critical Errors. So Level 3 would classify as Justification Gap.\n\nAlso, the solution says \"The families {L_j} and {R_j} are pairwise disjoint; moreover L_j ∩ R_k = ∅ for all j,k because L_j lies strictly below the diagonal while R_k lies strictly above it.\" This is correct.\n\nThus the only potential issues are minor missing details, but no critical errors.\n\nThus Level 3: only Justification Gaps (if any). So final verdict: PASS.\n\nNow we need to produce the JSON output with fields: Level 1 Result, Level 2 Result, Level 3 Result, Final Verdict, Reasoning.\n\nWe need to follow the output format: Should be JSON with keys: \"Level 1 Result\", \"Level 2 Result\", \"Level 3 Result\", \"Final Verdict\", \"Reasoning\". Possibly also \"Issues\" for Level 3. Let's see the instruction: \"Your response should provide a concise summary:\n\n* **Level 1 Result:** State whether the final answer is CORRECT or WRONG (quote the answer)\n* **Level 2 Result:** State whether reasoning uses VALID or INVALID mathematical principles\n* **Level 3 Result:** List any presentation issues found (Justification Gaps or Critical Errors in logic chain)\n* **Final Verdict:** \"PASS\" or \"FAIL\" based on the hierarchical decision tree\n* **Reasoning:** Brief explanation of the verdict\n\nAll detailed mathematical analysis should be provided in the structured JSON output (not in a separate prose log).\"\n\nThus we need to produce JSON with those fields. The Level 3 Result should be a list of issues. If none, we can say [] or \"None\". But we need to list any Justification Gaps. Let's identify any justification gaps.\n\nPotential justification gaps:\n\n- In Section 2, the argument that a tile covering a left-type square in column j must have rightmost column exactly j is given but not fully justified; they say \"d must be the largest column that can be used while staying left of all holes in [a,b]; hence d=j.\" This is a minor gap but not critical.\n\n- In Section 3, the claim that each column j (2..n-1) forces two distinct tiles is based on the previous claim; the distinctness is justified by the earlier statement that a tile cannot contain both left-type and right-type squares in the same column. That is adequately explained but could be considered a minor gap in the reasoning about the impossibility of a tile covering both types; but they gave a short justification: \"because that would force the hole (i,p(i)) to belong to [c,d]\". That's essentially the argument we gave earlier. It's fine.\n\n- In the construction, they claim the families {L_j} and {R_j} are pairwise disjoint; they give a brief justification. That's fine.\n\n- The solution does not explicitly verify that the tiles are disjoint (non-overlapping). They claim \"pairwise disjoint\" and \"L_j ∩ R_k = ∅\". However, they didn't explicitly argue that L_j and L_{j'} are disjoint for j ≠ j', but that's obvious because they are in different columns. Similarly for R_j and R_{j'}. So it's fine.\n\nThus any missing details are minor, thus Justification Gaps.\n\nThus Level 3 Result: Justification Gaps: e.g., \"The argument that a tile covering a left-type square in column j must have rightmost column exactly j is not fully detailed.\" \"The distinctness of tiles for each column is based on a brief justification that a tile cannot contain both left- and right-type squares in the same column; the reasoning could be expanded.\"\n\nBut we need to list them succinctly.\n\nNow produce JSON.\n\nWe need to ensure the output is within 2000 tokens. That's fine.\n\nThus final answer: PASS.\n\nNow produce JSON.\n\n",
