ht-type square in column j has leftmost column exactly j.\n\nThus each column j (2 <= j <= n-1) has both left-type and right-type squares, so there must be a tile with rightmost column j (cover left-type squares) and a different tile with leftmost column j (cover right-type squares). So each column j in 2..n-1 forces two distinct tiles. For j=1, only left-type squares, need tile with rightmost column 1. For j=n, only right-type squares, need tile with leftmost column n. Summing yields total tiles >= 2(n-2)+2 = 2(n-1). So lower bound.\n\nConstruction: Choose identity permutation p(i)=i, uncovered squares are diagonal cells. For each j in 1..n-1 define L_j = {(i,j) | i=j+1,...,n}. That's a vertical rectangle width 1, height n-j, rightmost column j, covering left-region squares of column j. For each j in 2..n define R_j = {(i,j) | i=1,...,j-1}. That's vertical rectangle width 1, height j-1, leftmost column j, covering right-region squares of column j. The families are disjoint, cover all squares except diagonal, each row and column has exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal). Number of tiles = (n-1)+(n-1) = 2(n-1). So bound is tight.\n\nThus answer for n=2025 is 2(2025-1) = 4048.\n\nThus solution appears correct.\n\nNow we need to apply hierarchical decision tree.\n\nLevel 1: Check answer correctness. The final answer is 4048. Is that correct? Let's verify quickly: The problem asks for minimum number of tiles. The solution claims lower bound 2(n-1) and construction achieving it, so answer is 2(2025-1)=4048. That seems plausible. We need to confirm that no better arrangement exists. The argument seems sound: each column j (2..n-1) forces two distinct tiles, plus columns 1 and n each force one tile, total 2n-2 = 2(n-1). So answer is correct. So Level 1: CORRECT.\n\nLevel 2: Check reasoning validity. We need to examine methods used. The solution uses:\n\n- Modeling uncovered squares as a permutation (valid combinatorial modeling).\n- Analyzing geometry of a tile: case