n the tile could have rightmost column up to s-1, which could be > j. But they claim d=j. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nSuppose we have a tile covering rows i in [a,b] and column j. For each row i, p(i) > j (since left-type). The tile covers columns from c to d, where c <= j <= d. For each row i, we need d < p(i). So d must be less than the minimum p(i) over i in [a,b]. So d <= min_i p(i) - 1. Since p(i) > j for all i, min_i p(i) >= j+1, so d <= min_i p(i) - 1 >= j. So d could be j or greater, but must be less than min p(i). For the tile to include column j, we need c <= j <= d. So d could be > j. But the argument says d must be exactly j because otherwise the tile would contain a square in column > j that might be left-type? Actually the tile covers squares in column j and possibly columns > j up to d. But for those columns > j, the squares would be left-type squares for those rows (since p(i) > j). However, for columns > j, they might be left-type squares for some rows but could be right-type for others? Let's examine: For any column j' > j, the set of rows where p(i) > j' is L_{j'}. Since p(i) > j for all rows in [a,b], but we don't know if p(i) > j' for all rows. Some rows may have p(i) = j+1, which is > j but not > j+1. So for column j+1, some rows may have p(i) = j+1, which would be not left-type (since p(i) = j+1 > j+1? Actually left-type for column j+1 means p(i) > j+1. So rows where p(i) = j+1 would not be left-type for column j+1; they'd be right-type? Wait, right-type for column j+1 means p(i) < j+1. If p(i) = j+1, then it's neither left nor right type; it's the hole column for that row. So tile cannot include column j+1 for that row because that would contain the uncovered square. So if any row i in [a,b] has p(i) = j+1, then tile cannot extend to column j+1 because that would include the uncovered square for that row. So d must be less than min_i p(i). So if min_i p(i) = j+1, then d <= j. So d = j. If min_i p(i) > j+1, then d could be > j. But the ti