olumn.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide, so the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nIn each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) there is exactly one unit square that is left uncovered; denote its column by \\(\\pi(i)\\).  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to the diagonal  \n\nPermute the columns of the board by the permutation \\(\\sigma=\\pi^{-1}\\).  \nAfter this column permutation the uncovered squares become  \n\n\\[\n(i,\\sigma(\\pi(i)))=(i,i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nA rectangle is mapped to a rectangle of the same size, and the condition “each unit square is covered by at most one tile’’ is preserved.  \nConsequently the minimal number of tiles for the original permutation \\(\\pi\\) equals the minimal number for the diagonal permutation.  \nHence we may (and shall) assume from now on that the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Critical cells  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\Bigl\\{(i,i+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\Bigr\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n   \\Bigl\\{(i+1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe first set consists of the cells **directly above** the diagonal, the second set of the cells **directly below** it.  \nClearly \\(|S|=2(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Lemma – a rectangle contains at most one cell of \\(S\\)\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let \\(R\\) be a rectangle that does **not** contain any diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\).  \n> Then \\(R\\) contains at most one element of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to obtain a contradiction, that \\(R\\) contains two distinct cells of \\(S\\).  \nThere are three possibilities for the pair:\n\n*Both are above‑diagonal cells.*  \nThen they have the form \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \nWithout loss of generality \\(i<j\\).  \nSince \\(R\\) contains the two cells, its row interval contains the rows \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) and its column interval contains the columns \\(i+1\\) and \\(j+1\\).  \nConsequently the whole \\(2\\times2\\) block formed by rows \\(i,i+1\\) and columns \\(i,i+1\\) lies inside \\(R\\); in particular the diagonal cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) belongs to \\(R\\), contradicting the hypothesis.\n\n*Both are below‑diagonal cells.*  \nThe argument is symmetric, using the block formed by rows \\(i,i+1\\) and columns \\(i,i+1\\) for the pair \\((i+1,i)\\) and \\((j+1,j)\\).\n\n*One cell is above the diagonal, the other below.*  \nThen the pair is \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j+1,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \nIf \\(i<j\\) the rectangle contains rows \\(i\\) and \\(j+1\\) and columns \\(i+1\\) and \\(j\\).  \nAgain the rows \\(i,i+1\\) together with the columns \\(i,i+1\\) form a \\(2\\times2\\) block that is a subset of \\(R\\); its lower‑right cell is the diagonal square \\((i+1,i+1)\\), a contradiction.  \nThe case \\(i>j\\) is analogous.\n\nThus any rectangle avoiding diagonal squares can contain **at most one** element of \\(S\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Lower bound for the number of tiles  \n\nEvery tile is a rectangle that does not cover any uncovered (diagonal) square, hence by the lemma it can contain at most one element of \\(S\\).  \nSince all cells of \\(S\\) must be covered, at least \\(|S|=2n-2\\) tiles are necessary.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the whole part of row \\(i\\) that lies **to the right** of the diagonal.  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the whole part of column \\(j\\) that lies **below** the diagonal.\n\nThe rectangles \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1},V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, because each of them stays strictly on one side of the diagonal.  \nTheir union covers every unit square except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\); consequently each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square, namely the diagonal one.\n\nThus a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, and the construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient.  "
