les are needed to cover \\(L\\).\n\n4. **Symmetric lower bound for \\(R\\).**  \n   By reflecting the board across the main diagonal, the same argument shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover \\(R\\).\n\n5. **Global lower bound.**  \n   Any admissible tiling uses at least  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n   tiles.\n\n6. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the uncovered squares on the main diagonal, i.e. \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \n   - For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a vertical rectangle  \n     \\[\n     V_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     of size \\((n-k)\\times1\\).  \n   - For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a horizontal rectangle  \n     \\[\n     H_k=\\{(k,j)\\mid j=k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     of size \\(1\\times(n-k)\\).\n\n   The rectangles \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover exactly the set \\(L\\) (all squares left of the diagonal).  \n   The rectangles \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover exactly the set \\(R\\) (all squares right of the diagonal).  \n   No rectangle contains a diagonal square, and every non\u2011diagonal square belongs to exactly one rectangle.  \n   Thus we obtain a tiling with precisely \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction uses \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the lower bound proves that no tiling can use fewer. Hence the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
