ion.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) consider the cell  \n\n\\[\nL_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1),\n\\]\n\nthe square immediately **to the left** of the uncovered one.  \nAny tile (axis‑parallel rectangle) that contains \\(L_i\\) must also contain the whole row‑interval of that tile; in particular it contains the whole column interval of the tile.  \nIf the column interval of the tile extended to the right of column \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\), then column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) would belong to the tile, and because the tile’s row interval contains row \\(i\\) the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) would be covered – a contradiction.  \nHence the **rightmost column** of every tile covering \\(L_i\\) is exactly \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\).\n\nAnalogously, for a row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) the cell  \n\n\\[\nR_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\n\\]\n\n(the square immediately **to the right** of the uncovered one) forces the **leftmost column** of any tile covering it to be exactly \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\).\n\nThe numbers \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) (for those \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\)) run through the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhile the numbers \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\) (for those \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\)) run through  \n\n\\[\n\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently:\n\n* at least one tile is needed whose rightmost column equals each integer \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\);\n* at least one tile is needed whose leftmost column equals each integer \\(k\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nA tile cannot simultaneously have rightmost column \\(k\\) and leftmost column \\(\\ell>k\\); therefore the two families of tiles are disjoint.  \nThus any tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles. ∎  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nFor each column \\(c\\) let  \n\n\\[\nr_c=\\sigma^{-1