n\n\\[\nr_c=\\sigma^{-1}(c)\n\\]\n\nbe the row of the uncovered square in that column.  \n\nDefine the **upper region**\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,c)\\mid 1\\le i< r_c\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the set of all squares that lie **above** the uncovered squares, and the **lower region**\n\n\\[\nD=\\{(i,c)\\mid r_c< i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe set of all squares that lie **below** the uncovered squares.  \nClearly  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl([n]\\times[n]\\bigr)\\setminus\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\}=U\\cup D,\n\\]\n\nand \\(U\\cap D=\\varnothing\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  Tiling the upper region \\(U\\)\n\nFor each column \\(c\\) set  \n\n\\[\nh_c=r_c-1\\qquad(0\\le h_c\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause the numbers \\(r_c\\) form a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), the multiset \\(\\{h_c\\}\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\nScan the columns from left to right, maintaining the smallest height seen so far.  \nFormally, let  \n\n\\[\nm_0=n-1,\\qquad a_1=1,\n\\]\n\nand for \\(c=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nm_c=\\min\\{h_1,\\dots ,h_c\\}.\n\\]\n\nWhenever \\(m_c<m_{c-1}\\) (i.e. a new smaller height appears) we close a rectangle:\n\n\\[\nR^{\\text{up}}_{k}= \\{1,\\dots ,m_{c-1}\\}\\times\\{a_k,\\dots ,c-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(a_k\\) is the column where this rectangle started.  \nThen we start a new rectangle at column \\(c\\) (so \\(a_{k+1}=c\\)).  \n\nSince the sequence \\((m_c)\\) drops from \\(n-1\\) down to \\(0\\) exactly once for each value in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\), we obtain precisely \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  \nEach rectangle \\(R^{\\text{up}}_{k}\\) consists of a **prefix of rows** \\(\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) together with a **contiguous block of columns**; therefore it is a legal tile.  \nMoreover, for any column \\(c\\) belonging to the block, we have \\(h_c\\ge m\\), i.e. the uncovered square in that column lies strictly below row \\(m\\); consequently no uncovered square lies inside \\(R^{\\text{up}}_{k}\\).  \nThus the rectangles \\(\\{R^{\\text{up}}_{k}\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) partition the whole upper region \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  Tiling the lower region \\(D\\)\n\nDefine the lower heights  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c=n-r_c\\qquad(0\\le\\ell_c\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nAgain \\(\\{\\ell_c\\}\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nScanning the columns from left to right and recording the current minimum  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c^{\\min}= \\min\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_c\\},\n\\]\n\nwe close a rectangle each time the minimum drops.  \nWhen the minimum becomes a new value \\(t\\) at column \\(c\\), we close the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR^{\\text{down}}_{k}= \\{n-t+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{a_k,\\dots ,c-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere again \\(a_k\\) is the starting column of this block.  \nExactly the same argument as for the upper region shows that we obtain \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles, each a **suffix of rows** together with a contiguous column interval, and none of them contains an uncovered square.  \nThese rectangles partition the lower region \\(D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  The complete tiling\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{T}= \\{R^{\\text{up}}_{1},\\dots ,R^{\\text{up}}_{n-1},\n               R^{\\text{down}}_{1},\\dots ,R^{\\text{down}}_{n-1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, they are pairwise disjoint, and their union is \\(U\\cup D\\), i.e. the whole board except the \\(n\\) uncovered squares.  \nHence a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles always exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nSection\u202f1 proved that any tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and Section\u202f2 exhibited a tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
