e \\(a\\) is the first column of the current block) and start a new block. Because the minimum drops from \\(n-1\\) down to \\(0\\) exactly \\(n-1\\) times, this produces \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles that cover every cell of \\(L\\).  \n   – The same procedure applied to \\(R\\) (using the row‑lengths \\(n-\\sigma(r)\\), which also form \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\)) yields another \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles covering all of \\(R\\).  \n   – All rectangles are axis‑parallel, contain no uncovered square, and together cover every unit square except the \\(n\\) uncovered ones. Hence a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower‑bound argument shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles always suffice. For \\(n=2025\\) this number equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(\\sigma(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\ni.e. a permutation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Lower bound: at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that any tiling satisfying the conditions uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n**Base case \\(n=1\\).**  \nThe board consists of a single square, which must be the uncovered one; no tile is needed.  \n\\(2\\cdot1-2=0\\), so the statement holds.\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the statement true for all sizes \\(<n\\) with \\(n\\ge2\\).  \nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, there exist rows\n\\[\ni\\quad\\text{with}\\quad\\sigma(i)=1,\\qquad\nj\\quad\\text{with}\\quad\\sigma(j)=n .\n\\]\n(The rows \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) are distinct, for a single row cannot contain two different co