at the rectangles are disjoint across the families (i.e., T_i and S_j cannot intersect). But they are in different triangles, so they are disjoint. That's fine.\n\nPotential issues: The solution assumes that the uncovered squares can be permuted to the diagonal by permuting rows and columns, which does not affect the number of tiles needed. That's a valid argument: we can reorder rows and columns arbitrarily without affecting tile count because tiles are axis-aligned and we can permute rows/columns globally. However, does permuting rows and columns preserve the property that each tile lies on grid lines? Yes, because we reorder the entire grid, not the tiles individually. But we need to be careful: If we permute rows and columns, we are effectively applying a permutation to the grid, which is a bijection on rows and columns. Tiles are axis-aligned rectangles; after permuting rows and columns, the shape of each tile may change (the rectangle may become a shape that is not a rectangle in the new coordinate system). Actually, if we permute rows arbitrarily, the grid lines are rearranged, and a rectangle defined by rows r1 to r2 and columns c1 to c2 will become a shape that may not be a rectangle because the rows are not contiguous after permutation. However, the solution says \"by permuting rows and columns we may assume sigma is identity, i.e., the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells. This does not change the number of tiles needed.\" Is that valid? Let's examine.\n\nWe have a grid of unit squares with rows indexed 1..n and columns 1..n. Tiles are axis-aligned rectangles whose sides lie on grid lines, i.e., they are defined by intervals of rows and columns: [r1,r2] x [c1,c2] (with r1 <= r2, c1 <= c2). If we permute rows (i.e., reorder the rows), the shape of a tile may become a union of disjoint row intervals, not a rectangle. For example, suppose we have a tile covering rows 1-3 and columns 2-4. If we swap rows 2 and 5, the tile now covers rows {1,5,3} which is not contiguous. So after arbitrary row permutation, the tile may no longer be a rectangle. So the claim that we can permute rows and columns without affecting the number of tiles needed might be false. However, the problem asks for the minimum number of tiles needed to achieve the condition. If we can permute rows and columns arbitrarily, we might be able to transform any configuration into one where uncovered squares are on diagonal, but the number of tiles needed for the transformed configuration may be different because the shape constraints changed. But the solution uses this as a reduction: they assume we can w.l.o.g. assume uncovered squares are diagonal. But is that a valid reduction? Let's think.\n\nThe condition is that each row and each column has exactly one uncovered square. That means the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix. The problem is symmetric under row and column permutations: If we have a tiling that satisfies the condition for a given permutation sigma, we can apply the same permutation to rows and columns of the entire grid, and also apply the same permutation to the tile positions (i.e., permute the rows and columns of each tile accordingly). Since the permutation is applied globally, each tile remains a rectangle because we permute the coordinate axes: we reorder the rows, but we also reorder the tile definitions accordingly. In other words, we can transform the whole configuration by applying a permutation to the grid: we rename row i to sigma(i), column j to sigma(j). The tiles are defined by intervals of rows and columns; after applying the same permutation to the row indices of each tile, the tile will still be a rectangle because the permutation is applied to both endpoints. However, we need to ensure that the permutation is monotonic? Actually, a permutation of indices does not preserve contiguity: the interval [r1,r2] after applying a permutation to each index yields a set of rows {sigma(r) | r1 <= r <= r2}. Thi