otal.  \n\nSince the lower bound and the construction coincide, the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2(n-1)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) let \\(\\sigma(i)\\) be the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nDefine the two disjoint sets of covered cells  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\n\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;\\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose interior consists only of cells from \\(L\\) or only of cells from \\(R\\); a rectangle cannot contain an uncovered cell, hence it cannot intersect both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A lower bound for the left region\n\nFor each column index \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_c=\\{\\,i\\mid c<\\sigma(i)\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, exactly the numbers \\(c+1,c+2,\\dots ,n\\) appear among the values \\(\\sigma(i)\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n|S_c|=n-c\\qquad (1\\le c\\le n-1).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(S_c\\) as a disjoint union of its maximal consecutive blocks of rows:\n\\[\nS_c=B_{c,1}\\cup B_{c,2}\\cup\\cdots\\cup B_{c,b_c},\n\\qquad\nb_c\\ge1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Claim.*  If a tile \\(T\\) covers a cell \\((i,c)\\in L\\) then the rightmost column of \\(T\\) is exactly \\(c\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  Let the rightmost column of \\(T\\) be \\(c'\\).  \nBecause \\((i,c)\\in L\\) we have \\(c<\\sigma(i)\\).  \n\n*If \\(c'>c\\):* then \\(T\\) also contains the cell \\((i,c+1)\\).  \nIf \\(\\sigma(i)=c+1\\) this cell is uncovered – contradiction.  \nIf \\(\\sigma(i)>c+1\\) then \\((i,c+1)\\in L\\), which is allowed, but then the same tile would also contain \\((i,c+2)\\), etc., until it reaches column \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\).  In particular, the tile would contain a cell in column \\(c'\\) for the same row \\(i\\).  Since \\(c'<\\sigma(i)\\) this is still in \\(L\\), so no contradiction yet.  However, consider any row \\(i'\\) belonging to the same tile.  Because the tile’s rows form a consecutive interval, there exists a row \\(i'\\) with \\(\\sigma(i')\\le c\\) (otherwise every row in the interval would have \\(\\sigma(\\cdot)>c\\) and the whole interval would be contained in \\(S_c\\); then the rightmost column could be reduced to \\(c\\) without losing any covered cell, contradicting the definition of the rightmost column).  For such a row \\(i'\\) the cell \\((i',c+1)\\) is either uncovered (\\(\\sigma(i')=c+1\\)) or lies in the right region (\\(\\sigma(i')\\le c\\)), both impossible for a tile confined to \\(L\\).  Hence \\(c'\\) cannot exceed \\(c\\).\n\n*If \\(c'<c\\):* then the tile does not contain the cell \\((i,c)\\), contrary to assumption.  \n\nThus \\(c'=c\\).  ∎\n\nConsequently, every tile covering any cell of column \\(c\\) has rightmost column \\(c\\).  Moreover, a tile cannot cover two distinct blocks of \\(S_c\\), because the rows of a rectangle are consecutive.  Hence each block \\(B_{c,k}\\) in the decomposition (2) requires a distinct tile.  In particular, at least one tile is needed for each column \\(c\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nT_L\\ge\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}1=n-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A lower bound for the right region\n\nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_c=\\{\\,i\\mid \\sigma(i)<c\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nExactly the numbers \\(1,2,\\dots ,c-1\\) appear among the values \\(\\sigma(i)\\), so  \n\n\\[\n|T_c|=c-1\\qquad (2\\le c\\le n).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nDecompose \\(T_c\\) into maximal consecutive blocks\n\\[\nT_c=C_{c,1}\\cup C_{c,2}\\cup\\cdots\\cup C_{c,d_c},\n\\qquad d_c\\ge1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Claim.*  If a tile \\(T\\) covers a cell \\((i,c)\\in R\\) then the leftmost column of \\(T\\) is exactly \\(c\\).\n\n*Proof.*  The argument is symmetric to the previous claim.  Since \\((i,c)\\in R\\) we have \\(\\sigma(i)<c\\).  If the leftmost column were \\(c'<c\\), the tile would also contain \\((i,c-1)\\), which is either uncovered (\\(\\sigma(i)=c-1\\)) or belongs to \\(L\\) (\\(\\sigma(i)<c-1\\)), both impossible.  Hence the leftmost column must be \\(c\\). ∎\n\nThus each block \\(C_{c,k}\\) of (5) needs a distinct tile, and in particular at least one tile is required for each column \\(c\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nT_R\\ge\\sum_{c=2}^{n}1=n-1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Global lower bound\n\nEvery tile belongs either to the left or to the right region, so the total number of tiles satisfies\n\n\\[\nT=T_L+T_R\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are precisely the main diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left region.*  \\(L=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\}\\).  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) the cells of \\(L\\) in that column are\n\\[\n\\{(i,c)\\mid i=c+1,c+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nwhich form a single consecutive vertical strip.  Tile this strip as a rectangle of size \\((n-c)\\times1\\).\n\n*Right region.*  \\(R=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\}\\).  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) the cells of \\(R\\) in that column are\n\\[\n\\{(i,c)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,c-1\\},\n\\]\nagain a single vertical strip, tiled as a rectangle of size \\((c-1)\\times1\\).\n\nThus we use exactly \\(n-1\\) tiles for \\(L\\) and \\(n-1\\) tiles for \\(R\\), i.e. \\(2n-2\\) tiles in total.  No tile contains a diagonal (uncovered) square, and the tiles are pairwise disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (7) and the explicit construction both give the same number of tiles.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]"
