ce the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is at column \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  Thus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll other unit squares must be covered by the tiles.  A tile is an axis\u2011parallel rectangle whose interior consists of a set of whole unit squares; different tiles are disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A large set of forced squares\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_A=\\{(i,\\sigma(i+1))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\qquad\nS_B=\\{(i+1,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n\\]\n\nand let  \n\n\\[\nS=S_A\\cup S_B .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Claim 1.*  The set \\(S\\) contains exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) distinct unit squares, none of which belongs to \\(U\\).\n\n*Proof.*  For each \\(i\\) the two squares \\((i,\\sigma(i+1))\\) and \\((i+1,\\sigma(i))\\) are different because \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\neq\\sigma(i)\\) (permutations have distinct values).  If \\((i,\\sigma(i+1))=(j,\\sigma(j+1))\\) then \\(i=j\\) and \\(\\sigma(i+1)=\\sigma(j+1)\\); similarly for two squares of type \\(S_B\\).  Suppose \\((i,\\sigma(i+1))=(j+1,\\sigma(j))\\).  Then \\(i=j+1\\) and \\(\\sigma(i+1)=\\sigma(j)\\); substituting \\(i=j+1\\) gives \\(\\sigma(j+2)=\\sigma(j)\\), impossible for a permutation.  Hence all elements of \\(S\\) are distinct, and \\(|S|=2(n-1)\\).  Moreover, a square of the form \\((i,\\sigma(i+1))\\) is not of the form \\((k,\\sigma(k))\\) because its column is \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\neq\\sigma(i)\\); similarly for \\((i+1,\\sigma(i))\\).  Thus \\(S\\cap U=\\varnothing\\). \u220e\n\nConsequently every tiling must cover all squares of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A fooling\u2011set lemma\n\nWe prove that a single tile cannot contain two different squares of \\(S\\).\n\n*Lemma.*  Let \\(R\\) be an axis\u2011parallel rectangle that contains no square of \\(U\\).  Then \\(|R\\cap S|\\le 1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose, to obtain a contradiction, that \\(R\\) contains two distinct squares of \\(S\\).  There are three possibilities.\n\n1. **Both squares belong to \\(S_A\\).**  \n   Let the squares be \\((i,\\sigma(i+1))\\) and \\((j,\\sigma(j+1))\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \n   The row interval of \\(R\\) contains the rows \\(i,\\dots ,j\\); in particular it contains row \\(i+1\\).  \n   The column interval of \\(R\\) contains the columns \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) and \\(\\sigma(j+1)\\); therefore it also contains column \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\).  \n   Hence the cell \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\) lies in \\(R\\).  But \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\in U\\) by (1), contradicting the assumption that \\(R\\) avoids all uncovered squares.\n\n2. **Both squares belong to \\(S_B\\).**  \n   Let the squares be \\((i+1,\\sigma(i))\\) and \\((j+1,\\sigma(j))\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \n   The row interval of \\(R\\) contains rows \\(i+1,\\dots ,j+1\\); in particular it contains row \\(j\\).  \n   The column interval contains \\(\\sigma(i)\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)\\); thus it contains column \\(\\sigma(j)\\).  \n   Consequently \\((j,\\sigma(j))\\in R\\), which is a member of \\(U\\), a contradiction.\n\n3. **One square from \\(S_A\\) and one from \\(S_B\\).**  \n   Let the squares be \\((i,\\sigma(i+1))\\) (type \\(S_A\\)) and \\((j+1,\\sigma(j))\\) (type \\(S_B\\)).  \n   Without loss of generality assume \\(i\\le j\\).  \n   Then the row interval of \\(R\\) contains rows \\(i,\\dots ,j+1\\); in particular it contains row \\(i+1\\).  \n   The column interval contains \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)\\); therefore it contains column \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\).  \n   Hence \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\in R\\), which again belongs to \\(U\\).  \n\nIn every case the existence of two distinct squares of \\(S\\) forces \\(R\\) to contain a hole, contradicting the hypothesis.  Thus any hole\u2011free rectangle can contain at most one element of \\(S\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nEvery tile is a hole\u2011free rectangle, so by the Lemma it can cover at most one square of \\(S\\).  Since all \\(2(n-1)\\) squares of \\(S\\) must be covered, any tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n|S|=2(n-1)\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ntiles.  For \\(n=2025\\) this gives the lower bound \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  The uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\(\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n*Tiles covering the region below the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{\\text{low}}=\\{(i,c)\\mid i=c+1,c+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\((n-c)\\times1\\).  These \\(n-1\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover every cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<i\\).\n\n*Tiles covering the region above the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{\\text{up}}=\\{(i,c)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,c-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\((c-1)\\times1\\).  These \\(n-1\\) rectangles are disjoint and cover every cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j>i\\).\n\nThe two families use exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles, they do not overlap, and none of them contains a diagonal (uncovered) square.  Hence a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (3) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]"
