ruction above achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   – Both the lower bound and the explicit construction give the same number, so the minimum possible number of tiles for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid is \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(p(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map \\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a permutation.\n\nA tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose interior consists solely of covered unit squares; consequently a rectangle may not contain any square \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal, i.e. take \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe set of covered squares splits into two monotone regions:\n\n* **Right of the diagonal:**  \n  \\[\n  R=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;i<j\\}.\n  \\]\n\n* **Left of the diagonal:**  \n  \\[\n  L=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;i>j\\}.\n  \\]\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;k<j\\le n\\},\n\\qquad\nL_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid k<i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(R_k\\) and \\(L_k\\) are rectangles of unit squares, they are pairwise disjoint, and  \n\n\\[\nR=\\bigcup_{k=1}^{n-1}R_k,\\qquad\nL=\\bigcup_{k=1}^{n-1}L_k .\n\\]\n\nThus the whole board is tiled by the \\(2(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles\n\\(\\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{L_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\).  \nHence\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles (induction)\n\nWe prove that **any** tiling of an \\(n\\times n\\) board satisfying the\nconditions uses at least \\(2n-2\\) til